consiSt("ak",{"courts":[{"l":"HIGHEST COURT","n":"Alaska Supreme Court","d":"Final appellate jurisdiction in all actions. Civil appeals of right from the superior court go straight here (there is no intermediate civil appeals court), as do superior-court decisions on administrative appeals. Reviews the Court of Appeals, and superior-court decisions on civil appeals from district court, only by discretionary petition for hearing. Also adopts the court rules and regulates the bar."},{"l":"INTERMEDIATE","n":"Alaska Court of Appeals","d":"Four judges. Hears criminal, post-conviction, juvenile delinquency (AS 47.12), extradition, habeas corpus, probation and parole, and bail appeals, plus excessive-sentence appeals. It does not hear ordinary civil appeals."},{"l":"TRIAL COURTS","n":"Superior Court (four judicial districts)","d":"One statewide trial court of general jurisdiction, organized in the First (Southeast), Second, Third, and Fourth Judicial Districts. Original jurisdiction in all civil and criminal matters, including probate and guardianship, and appellate jurisdiction over district court and agency appeals. A case within the district court’s concurrent jurisdiction may not be filed in superior court except as supreme court rules allow (protective-order petitions excepted)."},{"l":"LIMITED JURISDICTION","n":"District Court and magistrate judges","d":"A district court sits in each judicial district. Civil jurisdiction covers money claims up to $100,000 per defendant (exclusive of costs, interest, and fees), personal property, penalties, and evictions up to $100,000, plus misdemeanors. Claims up to $10,000 are heard as small claims on request. Magistrate judges handle many rural and limited matters."}],"slapp":[{"t":"No anti-SLAPP statute found in the Alaska Statutes","d":"Alaska has no special motion to strike, discovery stay, or speech-specific fee shifting in the statute chapters where such laws normally sit. AS 09.65 (Actions, Immunities, Defenses, and Duties) lists dozens of immunities, from emergency aid to defensive force, but none for speech or petitioning. AS 09.60 (Costs and Attorney Fees) has no SLAPP provision either, and no Title 9 chapter is devoted to the subject. Defend speech suits with ordinary Rule 12(b)(6) and Rule 56 motions. Civil Rule 82 fee shifting (and Rule 68 offers) still give the prevailing defendant partial fees, and AS 09.60.010(c) protects non-frivolous constitutional claimants (see the speech notes).\nThis was checked chapter by chapter, not by full-text search of the whole code. Before telling a client there is no protection, recheck the current Alaska Statutes and pending bills on akleg.gov.","c":"AS 09.65.050–.350 (no SLAPP section); AS 09.60.010–.230; Title 9 table of contents (2025 Alaska Statutes)","v":true}],"oos":[{"t":"Pro hac vice: Civil Rule 81(a)(2) motion, local counsel, Bar fee","d":"A member in good standing of a federal court bar or the highest court of any state or territory may appear in a particular case on motion. Serve the motion on the Alaska Bar Association’s executive director and on all parties. File it with (A) the name, address, and phone number of the associated Alaska Bar member, (B) that member’s signed written consent, (C) a good-standing and character certificate executed no earlier than 60 days before filing, and (D) proof that the Bar fee is paid.","c":"Alaska R. Civ. P. 81(a)(2)","v":true},{"t":"The fee is a full year’s active dues, per case, per year","d":"The Bar bylaws set the Civil Rule 81 fee at the annual active-member fee. It is owed per case per year until you notify the Bar that the case is closed or that you have withdrawn. The bylaws leave the dollar amount to the Board of Governors, so check the Bar’s current dues schedule.","c":"Alaska Bar Bylaws art. III, §§ 1(a), 4 (as amended through Oct. 15, 2026)","v":true},{"t":"Local counsel signs everything and outranks you","d":"You may take part in all respects, but every document that needs counsel’s signature must also bear local counsel’s signature. Local counsel is primarily responsible to the court for all stages of the case, and local counsel’s authority is superior to yours.","c":"Alaska R. Civ. P. 81(a)(2), (a)(3)","v":true},{"t":"Appellate courts: motion, or carry over the trial-court admission","d":"Out-of-state lawyers may be admitted in the Supreme Court or Court of Appeals on motion under Civil Rule 81(a)(2)–(3), and the clerk may decide the motion. If you were already admitted in the trial court in the same case, no motion is needed. File the trial court’s order, the good-standing certificate given to the trial court, and local counsel’s contact information with the appellate clerk.","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 103(b), (c)","v":true},{"t":"Limited-scope appearances are allowed in civil cases","d":"In a non-criminal case, a lawyer may appear for limited purposes, such as a deposition, hearing, discovery, or a motion. The entry of appearance must expressly say it is limited, define the limit by date, time period, or subject matter, and be served on all parties. When the limited task ends, withdraw without court approval by filing a notice certifying completion. The notice must give the client’s current service address, phone, and email, and you must give the client a list of pending deadlines and hearings. Otherwise, any appearance is for all purposes.","c":"Alaska R. Civ. P. 81(c)(1), (d), (e)(1)(D)","v":true},{"t":"TrueFiling e-filing is mandatory for lawyers","d":"TrueFiling is used in all trial court locations. All lawyers registered with the Alaska Bar must use it, and after a location goes live paper is accepted only during an outage. A filing made before 11:59 p.m. Alaska Time is filed that day, except protective-order and eviction petitions, which are filed the next business day if submitted after hours. Files must be under 10 MB, and a motion, memorandum, and exhibits go in a single document. Sign with “/s/ name” plus your Alaska Bar number.\nService between registered users runs through TrueFiling, which generates the certificate of service. Probate and guardianship cases were still on paper as of this writing; the court’s site says probate goes live November 30, 2026. The collected texts do not say whether the appellate courts use TrueFiling, so check with the Clerk of the Appellate Courts.","c":"Admin. Bull. No. 92 ¶¶ 1(a), 3, 7, 10(a), 13(a), 15, 17 (rev. Oct. 6, 2026); SCO 1943; courts.alaska.gov/efile","v":true},{"t":"Temporary practice without pro hac vice (Rule 5.5)","d":"Alaska follows the Model Rule 5.5 safe harbors. A lawyer admitted elsewhere may work temporarily in association with an actively participating Alaska lawyer, on matters related to a pending or expected proceeding where the lawyer is or expects to be authorized, on ADR related to the home practice, or on other work arising from the home practice. In-house and federally authorized work is also permitted. An office or systematic and continuous presence in Alaska requires admission.","c":"Alaska R. Prof. Conduct 5.5(b)–(e)","v":true}],"ethics":[{"t":"Governing code: Alaska Rules of Professional Conduct","d":"Adopted by Supreme Court order, mostly rescinded and repromulgated by SCO 1680 (effective April 15, 2009), and amended since, including SCO 1905 (2017) and SCO 1984 (2022). The structure follows the ABA Model Rules, with Alaska comments, but definitions appear at Rule 9.1 instead of 1.0, and Rules 7.4 (fields of practice) and 7.5 (firm names and letterheads) are still in force.","c":"Alaska R. Prof. Conduct (court PDF, current through 2026)","v":true},{"t":"Written disclosure if malpractice coverage is under $100,000/$300,000","d":"You must tell each client in writing if you lack malpractice insurance of at least $100,000 per claim and $300,000 annual aggregate, and again if coverage drops below those amounts or ends. Keep a record of the disclosures for six years after the representation ends. Salaried government lawyers and in-house counsel are exempt. The Model Rules have no such duty.","c":"Alaska R. Prof. Conduct 1.4(c)","v":true},{"t":"Written fee agreement over $1,000, with a fee-shifting warning","d":"If the fee will exceed $1,000, the basis or rate must be in a written fee agreement that describes the scope and includes the Rule 1.4(c) insurance disclosure. In litigation, the agreement must warn that the client may be liable for the opposing party’s costs and fees if the client loses (because of Civil Rule 82). Contingent-fee agreements must also state the costs and fees the client may owe the lawyer or the other side. The ABA rule only “preferably” requires writing for ordinary fees.","c":"Alaska R. Prof. Conduct 1.5(b), (c)","v":true},{"t":"Confidentiality uses “confidences and secrets,” with resolve-doubts-against-disclosure","d":"Rule 1.6 protects privileged “confidences” and “secrets.” A secret is other information that the client asked to keep confidential or whose disclosure would foreseeably embarrass or harm the client. Any uncertainty must be resolved against disclosure. The exceptions add preventing wrongful execution or incarceration of another and include the crime-fraud financial-harm exceptions. The Model Rule instead protects all “information relating to the representation.”","c":"Alaska R. Prof. Conduct 1.6(a), (b)","v":true},{"t":"No screening cure for lateral hires","d":"Alaska’s imputation rule has only the personal-interest exception. It lacks the Model Rule 1.10(a)(2) provision that lets a firm screen a newly hired lawyer’s former-client conflict, so a lateral’s conflict is imputed to the whole firm unless the affected client waives under Rule 1.7.","c":"Alaska R. Prof. Conduct 1.10(a), (c)","v":true},{"t":"Misconduct rule: harassment clause, no ‘prejudicial to justice’ catch-all","d":"Rule 8.4(f) bans knowing harassment or invidious discrimination toward court staff, other firms’ lawyers and staff, parties, witnesses, and seated jurors. Within the lawyer’s own firm, it applies only after a final agency or judicial finding of employment misconduct or discrimination. Alaska’s list has no Model Rule 8.4(d) ban on conduct “prejudicial to the administration of justice.” Its (d) is the improper-influence clause.","c":"Alaska R. Prof. Conduct 8.4(c), (d), (f), (g)","v":true},{"t":"Fee splitting between firms","d":"Lawyers in different firms may divide a fee only in proportion to each firm’s contribution, or if each firm assumes joint responsibility by written agreement with the client. The client must agree to each firm’s participation, including its share. Domestic-relations contingency fees and criminal-defense contingency fees are banned.","c":"Alaska R. Prof. Conduct 1.5(d), (e)","v":true}],"style":"State of Alaska","home":"https://courts.alaska.gov/","unique":{"mines":[{"t":"Losing costs you fees: Civil Rule 82","d":"Alaska shifts partial fees to the prevailing party in nearly every civil case. On a money judgment after a contested trial, the award is 20% of the first $25,000 and 10% of the rest. Without a money judgment, it is 30% (tried) or 20% (not tried) of reasonable actual fees. Budget for it, tell the client in the fee agreement (RPC 1.5(b)), and file your own motion within 10 days after the clerk’s distribution date on the judgment or the fees are waived.","c":"Alaska R. Civ. P. 82(b), (c); Alaska R. Prof. Conduct 1.5(b)"},{"t":"Ignoring a Rule 68 offer can cost up to 75% of actual fees","d":"If the final judgment is at least 5% worse for you than an offer you rejected (10% with multiple defendants), you pay all costs plus 75%, 50%, or 30% of the offeror’s reasonable actual fees from the offer date. The percentage depends on whether the offer was served within 60 days after the initial-disclosure date in the pretrial order, more than 90 days before trial, or later. This applies to plaintiffs and defendants alike.","c":"Alaska R. Civ. P. 68(b)"},{"t":"Five days to bump the judge","d":"The peremptory change of judge must be filed within 5 days after notice of assignment (or of your appearance, if you enter late), and before trial. Taking part in an evidentiary merits proceeding or a pretrial conference before the judge waives it.","c":"Alaska R. Civ. P. 42(c)(3), (4)"},{"t":"Answer in 20 days, not 21","d":"The answer is due 20 days after service (40 for the State, its officers and agencies, and some others). After a Rule 12 motion is denied, the answer is due 10 days after notice of the ruling.","c":"Alaska R. Civ. P. 12(a)"},{"t":"Clocks run from the clerk’s certificate of distribution","d":"Time to appeal and to seek reconsideration or review runs from the “date of notice.” For written orders, that is the date in the clerk’s certificate of distribution, not the date the judge signed. But a motion to alter or amend under Rule 59(f) must be served within 10 days after entry, and a new-trial motion within 10 days after the distribution date. Reconsideration under Rule 77(k) is due within 10 days, is limited to 5 pages, and is deemed denied after 30 days.","c":"Alaska R. Civ. P. 58.1(c), 59(b), (f), 77(k)"},{"t":"A notice of appeal needs a package, and fee proceedings don’t toll","d":"File in the Supreme Court within 30 days from the distribution date on the judgment. Include a docketing statement, the judgment, a statement of points on appeal, the fee or a waiver motion, a $750 cost bond (or supersedeas or deposit), a transcript designation, and proof of service. The clerk will refuse a notice that lacks these items. The appeal clock is not stopped while costs, prejudgment interest, or Rule 82 fees are being decided. Amend your points later to add the fee award.","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 204(a)(1), (a)(5), (b), (c)(1), (e)"},{"t":"Ten days to seek interlocutory review","d":"A petition for review of a non-appealable order is due 10 days after the date of notice of the order, with a 15-page limit. A timely reconsideration motion in the trial court restarts the period.","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 402, 403(a)(1)"},{"t":"Pro hac vice fee recurs every year","d":"The Civil Rule 81 fee is the full annual active-member dues, charged per case per year until you tell the Bar the case is closed or you have withdrawn. Local counsel must co-sign every filing.","c":"Alaska Bar Bylaws art. III, § 4; Alaska R. Civ. P. 81(a)(2)"},{"t":"Disclose thin malpractice coverage in writing","d":"If you appear in Alaska without malpractice coverage of at least $100,000 per claim and $300,000 aggregate, you must tell the client in writing. The disclosure belongs in any written fee agreement over $1,000.","c":"Alaska R. Prof. Conduct 1.4(c), 1.5(b)"},{"t":"Nonresident plaintiffs can be made to post security","d":"When the plaintiff lives out of state or is a foreign corporation, a defendant who demands it within 30 days after discovering the nonresidence stays the case until an undertaking of at least $200 is filed. The undertaking secures the costs and attorney fees that may be awarded against the plaintiff.","c":"AS 09.60.060"},{"t":"Wrong court for smaller cases","d":"Claims within the district court’s concurrent civil jurisdiction (up to $100,000 per defendant) may not be filed in superior court except as supreme court rules provide. Protective-order petitions are an exception. Check the rules before filing a smaller case in superior court.","c":"AS 22.10.020(a); AS 22.15.030(a)(1)"},{"t":"E-filing mechanics","d":"TrueFiling is mandatory for lawyers. Combine the motion, memorandum, and exhibits into one PDF, with the proposed order as a separate document in the same bundle. Files must be under 10 MB, and the cutoff is 11:59 p.m. Alaska Time.","c":"Admin. Bull. No. 92 ¶¶ 1(a), 7(a), 13(a)–(b), 15"}],"judge":[{"t":"One peremptory change of judge per side: 5 days, no reasons","d":"In superior and district court, each side gets one change of judge (and one of a master) as of right. Parties aligned on the same side count as one side, though the presiding judge may allow an extra change for a party adverse to a co-party. File a “Notice of Change of Judge” naming the judge, with no grounds and no affidavit, before trial starts and within 5 days after notice that the case is assigned to a specific judge. A party who enters later has 5 days from appearing or first pleading. A timely motion to disqualify for cause tolls the time for all parties, and a new 5-day period runs from notice of its denial.","c":"Alaska R. Civ. P. 42(c)(1)–(3)","v":true},{"t":"Waiver by participation","d":"You waive the peremptory change against a permanently assigned judge by knowingly taking part before that judge in a merits proceeding that considers evidence or affidavits, a pretrial conference, or the start of trial, or by agreeing to that judge. After a timely notice, the presiding judge reassigns within the district. If the new judge later becomes unavailable, the parties’ rights are restored as they stood before that assignment.","c":"Alaska R. Civ. P. 42(c)(4), (5)","v":true},{"t":"Statutory peremptory disqualification affidavit (AS 22.20.022)","d":"The statute separately lets a party or attorney file a good-faith affidavit that a fair and impartial trial cannot be had. The presiding judge then reassigns without proof. The affidavit is due within five days after the case is at issue on a question of fact or after assignment to a judge, whichever is later, unless good cause is shown. A party may file only one, and no more than two may be filed in an action. How this interacts with Rule 42(c) is not explained in the collected texts.","c":"AS 22.20.022(a), (c), (d)","v":true},{"t":"Disqualification for cause","d":"A judicial officer may not act where the officer is a party, a material witness, related within the third degree to a party or counsel, or has a direct financial interest (personally or through a spouse or child). Disqualification also applies if the officer represented or counseled a party, or opposed one, within two years, or was represented by a party’s attorney within two years, or if the officer’s former firm advised a party on the matter. Finally, the officer must step aside if a fair and impartial decision cannot be given. Grounds must be disclosed on the record, and some are waived if no one objects. If the judge denies a for-cause challenge, another judge assigned by the presiding judge of the next higher court decides it.","c":"AS 22.20.020(a)–(c)","v":true}],"speech":[{"t":"Constitutional claimants are shielded from fee shifting","d":"In a civil case or appeal to establish, protect, or enforce a federal or Alaska constitutional right, a claimant who prevails on the right gets full reasonable fees and costs for the constitutional claims. This applies only if the claimant lacked sufficient economic incentive to sue anyway. A losing claimant may not be ordered to pay the other side’s fees on the constitutional claims if the claim was not frivolous and the claimant lacked sufficient economic incentive. Courts may not vary fee awards based on the policy or interest a party advocates. This is the main fee-related protection for speech and petition claimants in the collected texts.","c":"AS 09.60.010(b)–(e)","v":true},{"t":"No speech-specific procedural shield","d":"No anti-SLAPP motion or other speech-specific early-dismissal procedure appears in the collected statutes or rules (see the anti-SLAPP entry). Alaska constitutional speech provisions were not collected and are not summarized here.","c":"AS 09.60, 09.65 (checked)","v":false}],"other":[{"t":"Rule 82: the loser pays part of the winner’s fees","d":"In civil cases the prevailing party is awarded partial fees on a schedule. For a money judgment with a contested trial, it is 20% of the first $25,000 and 10% of the rest. Contested without trial, it is 18%, 8%, 6%, and 2% across bands at $25,000, $100,000, and $500,000; non-contested, 10%, 3%, 2%, and 1%. With no money judgment, the award is 30% of reasonable actual fees if tried and 20% if not. The court may vary the award for listed factors, explaining why. The motion is due within 10 days after the date on the clerk’s certificate of distribution on the judgment, or the right is waived.","c":"Alaska R. Civ. P. 82(a)–(c)","v":true},{"t":"Rule 68 offers shift actual fees, both directions","d":"Either side may serve an offer more than 10 days before trial; it stays open 10 days. If the judgment is at least 5% less favorable to the offeree (10% with multiple defendants), the offeree pays costs plus 75%, 50%, or 30% of the offeror’s reasonable actual fees from the offer date, depending on how early the offer was served. The offeror gets the larger of this or the Rule 82 award, not both. Cases filed before August 7, 1997 use an older version.","c":"Alaska R. Civ. P. 68","v":true},{"t":"Tighter discovery than the federal rules","d":"Without a stipulation, each side (plaintiffs, defendants, third-party defendants) may take only three depositions of witnesses other than parties, independent trial experts, treating physicians, and necessary document custodians; more requires leave. Depositions are limited to 6 hours for parties, independent experts, and treating physicians, and 3 hours for others. Each party may serve 30 interrogatories on another party. Initial disclosures must also name potentially responsible persons for fault allocation under AS 09.17.080.","c":"Alaska R. Civ. P. 26(a)(1), 30(a)(2)(A), 30(d)(2), 33(a)","v":true},{"t":"Divorce cases: automatic financial disclosure in 45 days","d":"In divorce and legal separation actions, each party must disclose detailed financial information without a request within 45 days after the answer is filed. This includes signed releases, two years of account history, three years of tax returns, and recent pay stubs.","c":"Alaska R. Civ. P. 26.1","v":true}]},"appeals":{"asof":"November 10, 2026","src":"Alaska Rules of Appellate Procedure, Alaska Rules of Civil Procedure (Rules 58.1, 59, 62, 77, 79, 82), and Administrative Bulletin No. 92 (rev. Oct. 6, 2026), from the Alaska Court System’s complete unannotated rules PDFs at courts.alaska.gov/rules (current through SCOs effective 2026); AS 22.05.010, 22.07.020–.030, 22.10.020 from the Alaska Legislature’s statutes site (2025 Alaska Statutes).","intro":{"t":"Appeals in Alaska: civil cases go straight to the Supreme Court, and the clock starts on the clerk’s distribution date","d":"Alaska’s Court of Appeals handles only criminal and related matters. A civil appeal from superior court goes directly to the Alaska Supreme Court as of right. A party gets only one appeal of right, so a district court civil case is appealed to the superior court, and further Supreme Court review is discretionary. Superior-court decisions on administrative appeals may be appealed to the Supreme Court as of right.\nMost deadlines run from the “date of notice”: for a written judgment or order, the date shown in the clerk’s certificate of distribution (Civil Rule 58.1(c)). Notices of appeal go to the Clerk of the Appellate Courts with a required package of documents and a $750 cost bond. Proceedings on costs and Rule 82 attorney’s fees do not stop the appeal clock.","c":"AS 22.05.010(a)–(d); AS 22.07.020; Alaska R. App. P. 202, 204; Alaska R. Civ. P. 58.1(c)"},"groups":[["Which court and what is appealable",[{"t":"Supreme Court: final superior-court judgments in civil cases","d":"An appeal lies to the Supreme Court from a final superior-court judgment, as AS 22.05.010 allows, and from final decisions of the Workers’ Compensation Appeals Commission. Criminal, post-conviction, delinquency, extradition, habeas, probation, and bail matters go to the Court of Appeals instead.","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 202(a), (b); AS 22.07.020"},{"t":"District court and agency appeals go first to superior court","d":"Appeal a district court judgment to the superior court within 30 days from the distribution date. Appeal an agency decision within 30 days after it is mailed or distributed to you. If you timely ask the agency to reconsider, the 30 days run from the reconsideration decision or the deemed denial, whichever is earlier. The agency clock does not start until the decision clearly says it is final and that you have 30 days to appeal.","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 602(a)(1)–(2)"},{"t":"Partial final judgments","d":"If you appeal a Civil Rule 54(b) partial final judgment, attach the 54(b) order to the notice of appeal. The brief’s jurisdictional statement must say whether the judgment is final as to all claims and parties.","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 204(b)(2), 212(c)(1)(D)"}]],["Starting the appeal",[{"t":"30 days from the clerk’s certificate of distribution","d":"File the notice of appeal with the Clerk of the Appellate Courts within 30 days from the date shown in the clerk’s certificate of distribution on the judgment. Shorter times apply under Rules 216–219, such as 10 days for extradition and criminal peremptory-challenge appeals. A notice filed after the decision is announced but before distribution is treated as filed on the distribution date.","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 204(a)(1), (a)(6), 216(d)(1)"},{"t":"The required package","d":"The notice must identify the appellant, the judgment, and the court. With it, file: (1) a docketing statement listing the parties; (2) a copy of the judgment (and any 54(b) order); (3) a statement of points on appeal; (4) the filing fee, a fee-waiver motion, or a motion to appeal at public expense; (5) the cost bond or deposit, a superior-court order approving a supersedeas bond (or the motion for one), or a motion to waive the bond; (6) a transcript designation, if any; and (7) proof of service on all parties. The clerk refuses a notice without these, except that you may move to extend time for the points and the designation.","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 204(b)"},{"t":"Statement of points limits the appeal","d":"The court considers only points in the statement and points it can decide without reviewing untranscribed parts of the electronic record. The statement may be supplemented on motion for cause.","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 204(e)"},{"t":"$750 cost bond","d":"In a civil case, file a $750 cost bond with the notice (no approval needed at that amount) unless a supersedeas bond is filed, the court sets a different amount, or you deposit cash. Criminal cases, indigent parties with appointed counsel, and certain employee benefit appeals are exempt.","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 204(c)"},{"t":"Cross-appeals and later appeals","d":"Once a timely notice is filed, any other party may appeal within 14 days of that filing or within 30 days of the distribution date, whichever expires last. All parties below are parties to the appeal. Non-appellants are appellees and may file a notice of non-participation, but they are still bound by the result.","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 204(a)(2), (g)"}]],["Tolling and post-judgment motions",[{"t":"Motions that restart the appeal clock","d":"A timely motion under Civil Rule 50(b), 52(b), or 59, or for reconsideration under Civil Rule 77(k), terminates the running of appeal time. The full 30 days start again from the date of notice of the order deciding it. For reconsideration, the period restarts on that date or on the Rule 77(k)(4) deemed-denial date, whichever is earlier.","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 204(a)(3)"},{"t":"Short trial-court deadlines feed the tolling","d":"A new-trial motion must be served within 10 days after the distribution date. A motion to alter or amend must be served within 10 days after entry of judgment. Reconsideration is due within 10 days after notice, is limited to 5 pages, and is deemed denied if not decided within 30 days.","c":"Alaska R. Civ. P. 59(b), (f), 77(k)"},{"t":"Costs and fee proceedings do not toll","d":"Time to appeal keeps running while costs are taxed, prejudgment interest is calculated, or Rule 82 fees are decided. Add those rulings by moving to amend your statement of points and transcript designation. If no appeal is pending, a post-judgment costs or fees order is separately appealable on those issues. The trial court keeps jurisdiction over costs and fees while the appeal is pending.","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 204(a)(5)(A)–(B), (a)(2)"}]],["Stays and settlement",[{"t":"Supersedeas bond in the superior court first","d":"To stay a money judgment, present a supersedeas bond to the superior court. It must cover the whole unsatisfied judgment plus appeal costs and interest unless the court, after notice and hearing for good cause, sets a different amount or other security. Enforcement is automatically held off for only 10 days after the distribution date. The Supreme Court normally considers a stay only after the trial court has denied one or granted it on other terms.","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 204(d), 205; Alaska R. Civ. P. 62"},{"t":"Mandatory settlement discussion in civil appeals","d":"Counsel for all parties in a civil appeal to the Supreme Court must discuss settlement by the date in the clerk’s opening notice, and the appellant’s lawyer reports to the clerk whether it happened and what settled. Self-represented appeals and certain other appeals are exempt. Any party may move for a settlement conference after the notice of appeal.","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 221, 222(a)"}]],["Briefing",[{"t":"Schedule: 30 / 30 / 20","d":"After the record and transcript are complete, the clerk sends notice of the due date. The appellant’s brief is due 30 days after that notice and the appellee’s 30 days after service of the appellant’s brief. Within 20 days after the appellee’s brief, the appellant files a reply or a notice that none will be filed. Separate briefs by multiple appellants or appellees get 10 extra days when excerpts are being prepared.","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 210(f), 212(a)(1)(A)"},{"t":"Page limits, not word counts","d":"Principal briefs are limited to 50 numbered pages, counted from the jurisdictional statement and excluding appendices, and replies to 20 pages. A combined appellee/cross-appellant brief is 50 pages, and a cross-appellee’s combined reply and response is 50, of which the reply part is at most 20. A motion to exceed the limits must attach the proposed over-length brief.","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 212(c)(3), (c)(4), (c)(6)"},{"t":"Format: 13-point serif or 12.5-point sans serif, one-inch side margins","d":"Use 8½ x 11 paper, double spaced, with text (including footnotes) of at least 13-point Times New Roman or similar, or 12.5-point Arial or similar. Brief side margins are one inch. File the original and one copy for a compliance check. Within ten days after the brief is returned, file bound copies (ten for the Supreme Court, seven for the Court of Appeals) and serve two on each party. Covers are ivory or tan for the appellant, blue for the appellee, green for replies, and red for amici.","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 212(a)(1)(B), (a)(2), (b), 513.5(b)–(c)"},{"t":"Contents that trip up newcomers","d":"For each issue, the statement of the case must show whether, when, and how it was raised and ruled on below. The argument must state the standard of review and, for unpreserved issues, address plain error. Property-division appeals need an asset-and-liability table. Avoid calling parties “appellant” and “appellee.”","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 212(c)(1)(G)–(J), (c)(7)"},{"t":"Extensions by notice","d":"By filing a notice on or before the due date, an appellant may take up to 30 days for the opening brief and 15 for the reply, and an appellee up to 30 days. Expedited matters are excluded. Beyond that, a motion must show diligence and substantial need (up to 30 or 15 more days), or extraordinary and compelling circumstances.","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 503.5(b), (c)"},{"t":"Oral argument must be requested","d":"In appeals as of right, oral argument is held only if requested no later than 10 days after the reply brief’s due date (or the appellee’s brief’s, if no reply is allowed).","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 505(a)(2)"}]],["Decision, rehearing, costs, and fees",[{"t":"Rehearing: 10 days, 5 pages","d":"A petition for rehearing is due within 10 days after the date of notice of the opinion and may not exceed five pages. It must specify the overlooked statute, decision, fact, or question. No response is received unless the court requests one.","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 506(a), (b)"},{"t":"No mandate; jurisdiction returns automatically","d":"The opinion or dispositive order is the judgment, and no mandate issues. In the Supreme Court, jurisdiction returns to the trial court the day after rehearing is decided, the day after the rehearing deadline passes, or the day after a petition for hearing is denied.","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 507(a)–(c)"},{"t":"Costs and attorney’s fees on appeal","d":"The prevailing party files a verified bill of costs within 10 days after the date of notice of the opinion, and objections are due 7 days later. Fees on appeal are awarded only if a statute, caselaw, or contract provides for them, the appeal is frivolous or in bad faith, or justice requires. In superior-court appeals under Part Six, the prevailing party gets 20% of actual fees. A fee motion with an affidavit is due within 10 days after notice of the opinion.","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 508(d)–(f)"},{"t":"Precedent quirks","d":"Issues decided by an equally divided court, or 2–1 when only three justices sit, have no precedential effect. Memorandum opinions and judgments may not be cited, according to the court’s appellate webpage.","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 106; courts.alaska.gov/appellate"}]],["Discretionary review",[{"t":"Petition for hearing (review of an intermediate appellate decision)","d":"To seek Supreme Court review of a Court of Appeals decision, or review of a superior-court appellate decision in a district court case, file within 30 days after the date of notice (or after notice of the rehearing ruling). The limit is 15 pages, with reasons tied to the Rule 304 criteria. Cross-petitions and responses are due 30 days after service. Any deadline may be extended up to 15 days by notice.","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 302, 303(a)–(d), 304"},{"t":"Petition for review of non-final orders: 10 days","d":"Review of an order that is not appealable is discretionary. It is granted only for listed reasons: injustice from postponement, an important contested question whose resolution would advance the case, a serious departure from usual procedure, or an issue that would otherwise evade review. File within 10 days after the date of notice (15-page limit). Cross-petitions and responses are due in 10 days. A timely reconsideration motion restarts the period.","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 402(b), 403(a)–(c)"},{"t":"Original applications and certified questions","d":"Original applications for relief and certified questions of state law from other courts are handled under Part Four.","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 404, 407"}]],["Filing mechanics and out-of-state counsel",[{"t":"Time computation and extensions","d":"Exclude the trigger day and roll past weekends and holidays. Add 3 calendar days for mail service, but no days for electronic service. Only the appellate courts, not the trial court, may extend appellate deadlines.","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 502(a)–(c)"},{"t":"Motions practice","d":"Oppositions to motions are due 7 days after service. Reconsideration of an order is due within 10 days after its date of notice.","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 503(d), (h)"},{"t":"Pro hac vice in the appellate courts","d":"Appear on motion under Civil Rule 81(a)(2)–(3), which the clerk may decide. If you were admitted below in the same case, file the trial court order, the good-standing certificate, and local counsel’s details instead.","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 103(b), (c)"},{"t":"E-filing in the appellate courts: confirm with the clerk","d":"Administrative Bulletin 92 governs TrueFiling for trial-court case types. The collected texts do not confirm whether, or how, appellate filings go through TrueFiling, and the rules still call for paper originals and bound copies. Confirm with the Clerk of the Appellate Courts before filing.","c":"Admin. Bull. No. 92; Alaska R. App. P. 212(a)"}]],["Deadlines",[{"t":"Notice of appeal (civil, to Supreme Court)","d":"30 days from date shown in the clerk’s certificate of distribution on the judgment.\nFile with the Clerk of the Appellate Courts with the full Rule 204(b) package and the $750 cost bond.","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 204(a)(1)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (date shown in the clerk’s certificate of distribution on the judgment)","note":"File with the Clerk of the Appellate Courts with the full Rule 204(b) package and the $750 cost bond."}},{"t":"Cross-appeal / subsequent appeal","d":"14 days from filing of another party’s timely notice of appeal (or 30 days from distribution, whichever is later).\nAlso the route to appeal a later costs or fees order.","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 204(a)(2)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (filing of another party’s timely notice of appeal)","note":"Also the route to appeal a later costs or fees order."}},{"t":"Appeal to superior court from district court","d":"30 days from date in the clerk’s certificate of distribution on the judgment.","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 602(a)(1)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (date in the clerk’s certificate of distribution on the judgment)","note":""}},{"t":"Appeal to superior court from an agency","d":"30 days from mailing or distribution of the final decision (or of the reconsideration ruling or deemed denial).\nThe clock starts only if the decision states it is final and that there are 30 days to appeal.","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 602(a)(2)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (mailing or distribution of the final decision)","note":"The clock starts only if the decision states it is final and that there are 30 days to appeal."}},{"t":"Motion for new trial","d":"10 days from date in the clerk’s certificate of distribution on the judgment.\nServed, not just filed. Tolls appeal time under App. R. 204(a)(3).","c":"Alaska R. Civ. P. 59(b)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (date in the clerk’s certificate of distribution on the judgment)","note":"Served, not just filed. Tolls appeal time under App. R. 204(a)(3)."}},{"t":"Motion to alter or amend judgment","d":"10 days from entry of judgment.\nMeasured from entry, not from the distribution date.","c":"Alaska R. Civ. P. 59(f)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Measured from entry, not from the distribution date."}},{"t":"Trial-court reconsideration","d":"10 days from date of notice of the ruling.\n5-page limit; deemed denied after 30 days; tolls appeal time.","c":"Alaska R. Civ. P. 77(k)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (date of notice of the ruling)","note":"5-page limit; deemed denied after 30 days; tolls appeal time."}},{"t":"Rule 82 attorney’s fee motion","d":"10 days from date in the clerk’s certificate of distribution on the judgment.\nWaived if late unless the court allows more time. Does not toll appeal time.","c":"Alaska R. Civ. P. 82(c)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (date in the clerk’s certificate of distribution on the judgment)","note":"Waived if late unless the court allows more time. Does not toll appeal time."}},{"t":"Cost bill in trial court","d":"10 days from date in the clerk’s certificate of distribution on the judgment.\nObjections due 7 days after service.","c":"Alaska R. Civ. P. 79","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (date in the clerk’s certificate of distribution on the judgment)","note":"Objections due 7 days after service."}},{"t":"Appellant’s opening brief","d":"30 days from clerk’s notice of the brief due date.\n50 numbered pages.","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 212(a)(1)(A)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (clerk’s notice of the brief due date)","note":"50 numbered pages."}},{"t":"Appellee’s brief","d":"30 days from service of appellant’s brief.\n50 numbered pages.","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 212(a)(1)(A)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (service of appellant’s brief)","note":"50 numbered pages."}},{"t":"Reply brief (or notice of no reply)","d":"20 days from service of appellee’s brief.\n20 pages.","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 212(a)(1)(A)","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (service of appellee’s brief)","note":"20 pages."}},{"t":"Request for oral argument","d":"10 days from due date of the reply brief (or of the appellee’s brief if no reply is allowed).\nNo argument without a timely request.","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 505(a)(2)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (due date of the reply brief)","note":"No argument without a timely request."}},{"t":"Petition for rehearing","d":"10 days from date of notice of the opinion or decision.\n5-page limit.","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 506(b)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (date of notice of the opinion or decision)","note":"5-page limit."}},{"t":"Petition for hearing","d":"30 days from date of notice of the intermediate appellate decision (or of the rehearing ruling).\n15 pages; response 30 days after service; extend up to 15 days by notice.","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 303(a)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (date of notice of the intermediate appellate decision)","note":"15 pages; response 30 days after service; extend up to 15 days by notice."}},{"t":"Petition for review (non-final order)","d":"10 days from date of notice of the order.\n15 pages; response and cross-petition 10 days after service.","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 403(a)(1)(A)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (date of notice of the order)","note":"15 pages; response and cross-petition 10 days after service."}},{"t":"Appellate cost bill / fee motion","d":"10 days from date of notice of the opinion or Rule 214 order.\nObjections to the cost bill are due within 7 days.","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 508(f)(1)–(2)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (date of notice of the opinion or Rule 214 order)","note":"Objections to the cost bill are due within 7 days."}},{"t":"Opposition to appellate motion","d":"7 days from service of the motion.","c":"Alaska R. App. P. 503(d)","due":{"n":7,"from":"the trigger date (service of the motion)","note":""}}]]],"courts":[],"links":[["Official site","https://courts.alaska.gov/"]]},"rulesName":"Alaska Rules of Civil Procedure","rulesSrc":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf","rulesAsof":"as checked November 10, 2026","rulesNote":"Each entry is our summary of the rule, not the rule itself. Tap Read the official text before you rely on it.","rules":[{"n":"1","t":"Scope and construction of rules","s":"These rules govern all civil proceedings (legal, equitable, or otherwise) in the superior court and, as far as they apply, in the district court. They are to be construed to secure the just, speedy, and inexpensive determination of every action.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"2","t":"One form of action","s":"There is only one form of action, called a “civil action.”","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"3","t":"Commencement of action and venue","s":"A civil action starts by filing a complaint, which the clerk will accept only with a completed case description form and (unless filed by fax or email where allowed) a postage-paid envelope addressed to the plaintiff for mailing the summonses. Real property actions (ejectment, quiet title, partition, lien enforcement, etc.) must be filed in the judicial district where the property lies; other actions may be filed where the claim arose or where the defendant can be personally served, with trial held at the most convenient venue district location, subject to a change-of-venue motion. Failing to object to venue in time waives the objection, and domestic violence, stalking, or sexual assault protective order petitions may be filed where the petitioner lives, where the respondent lives, or where the conduct occurred.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"4","t":"Process (summons and service)","s":"The clerk issues a summons on filing of the complaint, and process is served by a peace officer, a process server specially appointed by the Commissioner of Public Safety, or, where allowed, by registered or certified mail with restricted delivery (complete when the return receipt is signed); the rule lists how to serve individuals, minors, incompetent persons, corporations, partnerships, the State (by mail to the Attorney General in Juneau plus the Anchorage or Fairbanks office for Third or Fourth District cases), public corporations, and people outside the state or abroad. If a party cannot be found after diligent inquiry (including an internet search) shown by affidavit, service is by posting notice on the court system’s legal notice website for 4 consecutive weeks plus mailing by certified and regular mail before the last week, and the court may add methods such as newspaper publication once a week for 4 weeks, email, social media, or posting on a door; the notice must allow at least 20 days after personal service or 30 days after the last publication to respond. Within 120 days after filing the complaint, the plaintiff must file an affidavit listing who has and has not been served. Under Alaska’s 4(j), the clerk reviews each case at 120 days and sends a notice to show good cause for any unserved defendant, and if good cause is not shown within 30 days the action is dismissed without prejudice as to that defendant.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"5","t":"Service and filing of pleadings and other papers","s":"Papers after the complaint (orders, pleadings, discovery papers, written motions, notices, offers of judgment) must be served on every party, on the attorney if a party is represented, by hand delivery, first-class mail (complete on mailing), fax or email under Rule 5.1, or commercial delivery (complete on delivery). Papers must be filed before or within a reasonable time after service, but discovery materials, Rule 26(a) disclosures, subpoenas, offers of judgment, counsel correspondence, and exhibits are not filed until used. If a represented party is served with a post-judgment motion after 1 year has passed with no filing and the appeal time has run, the party personally (not the lawyer) must be served unless the lawyer filed a notice of continued representation. Parties must serve any custody investigator or guardian ad litem with custody-related pleadings and must immediately report changes of mailing address, email, and phone number in writing.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"5.1","t":"Filing and service by fax and email","s":"Documents may be filed by fax or email only as permitted by a presiding judge’s administrative order or with prior written consent of the assigned judge, and the filer must keep the original; filings received before 4:30 p.m. on a court business day are filed that day, and later ones on the next business day. Courts must accept faxed certified copies of out-of-state, tribal, or territorial domestic violence protective orders sent by the issuing court. Service by fax or email is allowed only on people who consented in writing (consent may be revoked), fax service is limited to 25 pages per recipient per day unless the parties agree otherwise (cover sheets excluded), and service completed after 4:30 p.m. counts as made at the start of the next business day.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"5.2","t":"Foreign orders and judgments","s":"When state law requires notice of registration of an out-of-state support order, income withholding order, or child custody determination, the court sends it by first-class mail unless the registering party asks for certified mail or personal service, and the time to request a hearing runs from mailing or service. The court mails notice of filing of a foreign judgment by first-class mail, and the creditor may mail an additional notice. Social security numbers must be kept out of the public record (with a redacted duplicate and a confidential information sheet), motions to enforce or modify registered support orders and to enforce custody orders may be served by first-class mail, but motions to modify a registered custody order require Rule 4 service.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"5.3","t":"Electronic distribution by the court","s":"The court system may email notices, orders, and judgments to attorneys, court-appointed professionals, and agencies, and self-represented parties may opt in (filing by email counts as opting in). Alaska bar members must keep a current email address with the Alaska Bar Association, agencies and appointed professionals register addresses with the court, and email changes must be reported immediately. Emailed distributions are treated as mailed for computing response deadlines, except no time is added when an order sets a specific date, and certain documents such as letters testamentary, divorce decrees, and adoption decrees are still sent as certified copies by first-class mail.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"5.4","t":"Registration of tribal court orders","s":"Any tribe, tribal organization, or person may ask the superior court to register a federally recognized tribe’s court order in a divorce, dissolution, custody, paternity, or name change case by filing a request, a copy of the order, a sworn statement that it has not been vacated, stayed, or modified, and contact information; the court mails these and a notice to the tribal court and the parties. A party who objects must request a hearing within 20 days after being served the notice. After a hearing or the 20-day period, the court must confirm registration by written order unless the procedure was not followed, the tribal court lacked jurisdiction, the order was vacated or modified, the objector lacked notice or a chance to be heard, or recognition would violate Alaska public policy; registered orders are enforced like Alaska orders.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"6","t":"Time","s":"When counting time, exclude the triggering day and include the last day unless it is a Saturday, Sunday, or legal holiday; for periods under 7 days, intermediate weekends and holidays are excluded. The court may extend a deadline for cause if asked before it expires, or after it expires for excusable neglect, but may not extend deadlines under Rules 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e), and (f), and 60(b) except as those rules allow. Three calendar days are added when a document is served or distributed by non-electronic mail (but not for Rule 4(h) service or when an order sets a specific date); no time is added for electronic service.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"7","t":"Pleadings allowed and form of motions","s":"The permitted pleadings are a complaint, answer, reply to a counterclaim, answer to a cross-claim, third-party complaint, and third-party answer; the court may order a reply to an answer. A request for a court order must be made by motion, in writing unless made at a hearing or trial, stating the grounds with particularity and the relief sought, and motion procedure is governed by Rule 77. Demurrers, pleas, and exceptions for insufficiency are abolished.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"8","t":"General rules of pleading","s":"A claim for relief needs a short and plain statement showing entitlement to relief and a demand for judgment, which may seek alternative or different types of relief. Defenses must be stated plainly and each allegation admitted or denied (lack of knowledge counts as a denial), affirmative defenses listed in the rule (such as statute of limitations, release, res judicata, contributory negligence, assumption of risk, and fraud) must be pleaded, and allegations not denied (other than damages) are admitted. Alternative and inconsistent claims or defenses are allowed, subject to Rule 11, and pleadings are construed to do substantial justice.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"9","t":"Pleading special matters","s":"A party need not plead capacity to sue or be sued except to show jurisdiction, and anyone challenging capacity or legal existence must do so by specific negative averment. Fraud and mistake must be pleaded with particularity, while intent and other states of mind may be alleged generally; conditions precedent may be alleged generally but must be denied specifically. Official documents, judgments, and statutes may be pleaded in summary form, time and place allegations are material, and special damages must be specifically stated.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"10","t":"Form of pleadings","s":"Every pleading needs a caption with the court, judicial district, city, case title, case number, and the Rule 7(a) designation, and the complaint must name all parties with as much of each full legal name as is known. Claims and defenses go in numbered paragraphs, separate claims and defenses in separate counts where that helps clarity, and attached written instruments are part of the pleading. A complaint, counterclaim, or cross-claim seeking relief under a specific statute must cite that statute in parentheses after the pleading title or section heading, and all pleadings must also follow Rule 76.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"11","t":"Signing of papers and representations to the court","s":"Every pleading, motion, and other paper must be signed by an attorney of record or by the unrepresented party and must list the signer’s address, email, and phone; an unsigned paper is struck unless promptly corrected, and verification is not required unless a rule or statute says so. By presenting a paper, the signer certifies after reasonable inquiry that it is not for an improper purpose, its legal contentions are warranted by law or a nonfrivolous argument, and its factual contentions and denials have or will likely have evidentiary support. Although titled “Sanctions,” the rule as printed contains only these two subsections and no sanctions or safe-harbor procedure like Federal Rule 11(c).","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"12","t":"Defenses and objections; motions on the pleadings","s":"A defendant must answer within 20 days after service of the summons and complaint (the same 20 days applies to answering a cross-claim or replying to a counterclaim), while the State, its officers and agencies, defendants served through a state agent, and individuals served in a foreign country get 40 days; Alaska’s 20-day period is shorter than the federal 21 days. If a Rule 12 motion is denied or deferred, or a more definite statement is ordered, the responsive pleading is due 10 days after notice of the ruling or service of the statement. Defenses such as lack of jurisdiction, improper venue, defective process or service, failure to state a claim, and failure to join a Rule 19 party may be raised by pre-answer motion; personal jurisdiction, venue, and process defenses are waived if omitted, and an order of a more definite statement must be obeyed within 10 days. A motion to strike may be made within 20 days when no response is allowed, and an order granting dismissal or judgment on the pleadings is not a final judgment—the court directs a party to file a separate proposed final judgment within 20 days of service of the decision.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"13","t":"Counterclaims and cross-claims","s":"A party must plead as a counterclaim any claim against an opposing party arising from the same transaction or occurrence, unless it is already pending in another action or the court lacks needed third parties, and may plead unrelated claims as permissive counterclaims, which may exceed or differ from the opposing claim. Counterclaims maturing after pleading, or omitted through oversight or excusable neglect, may be added with court permission, and cross-claims against co-parties are allowed for claims arising from the same transaction or property. The rules do not expand counterclaims against the State, additional parties may be joined under Rules 19 and 20, and separate judgments may enter under Rule 54(b).","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"14","t":"Third-party practice","s":"A defending party may serve a third-party complaint on a nonparty who may be liable for all or part of the plaintiff’s claim, without leave if filed within 10 days after serving the original answer and otherwise only by motion. The third-party defendant responds under Rules 12 and 13 and may assert defenses and related claims against the plaintiff, who may in turn assert related claims against the third-party defendant, and a plaintiff facing a counterclaim may also bring in third parties. Alaska-specific subsection (c) lets a defendant add any person whose fault may have caused the plaintiff’s damages for apportionment under AS 09.17.080, and judgment may be entered against that person for the plaintiff according to that person’s percentage of fault even without a direct claim.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"15","t":"Amended and supplemental pleadings","s":"A party may amend once as a matter of course before a responsive pleading is served, or within 20 days after service if no response is permitted and the case is not on the trial calendar; otherwise amendment requires the opponent’s written consent or leave of court, freely given when justice requires, and the response to an amended pleading is due within the remaining original time or 10 days after service, whichever is longer. Issues tried by consent are treated as pleaded, and amendments relate back if they arise from the same conduct, including a change of party if within the Rule 4(j) service period the new party had notice and knew it would have been sued but for a mistake of identity. The court may allow supplemental pleadings about later events, and an amended pleading must be retyped and filed complete in itself, with all exhibits, or it is not deemed amended.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"16","t":"Pretrial conferences, scheduling, and management","s":"The court may hold pretrial conferences to speed disposition, manage the case, improve trial preparation, and encourage settlement, including through mediation or other alternative dispute resolution. Except in exempt cases, the judge must issue a scheduling order within 90 days after the defendants appear (or as a local uniform pretrial order provides) setting deadlines to join parties, identify and join potentially responsible persons under AS 09.17.080, file motions, disclose experts, supplement disclosures, identify witnesses and exhibits, finish discovery, and set trial, and it may be modified only for good cause. Orders after a conference control the case, a final pretrial order may be changed only to prevent manifest injustice, and failing to obey scheduling orders or to appear or participate in good faith can bring Rule 37 sanctions plus mandatory expenses and attorney’s fees. Exempt from scheduling orders are Part XII special proceedings (including divorce and forcible entry and detainer), paternity, custody, small claims, enforcement of out-of-state judgments, eminent domain, post-conviction relief, and domestic violence and stalking protective order cases.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"16.1","t":"Special procedures for reducing litigation delay (rescinded)","s":"This rule has been rescinded and is no longer in effect.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"16.2","t":"Informal trials in domestic relations cases","s":"In divorce, property division, custody, and child support cases (including motions to modify), the parties and court may voluntarily agree to an informal trial, where the court may admit any relevant evidence even if inadmissible under formal evidence rules and normally only the parties testify. Each party speaks to the court under oath, only the judge questions the parties, documents and expert reports may be admitted without supporting testimony, and letters from children about custody preferences are discouraged. A party may withdraw the election only if the other side is not prejudiced (and not to delay trial absent good cause), and the court may switch the case to a formal trial at any time.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"17","t":"Parties plaintiff and defendant; capacity","s":"Every action must be brought in the name of the real party in interest, though executors, guardians, trustees, and others authorized by statute may sue in their own names, and no case is dismissed on this ground until there has been a reasonable time after objection for ratification, joinder, or substitution. An individual’s capacity to sue is set by the law of the person’s domicile, a corporation’s by the law where it was organized, and partnerships and associations may sue or be sued in their common name. Minors and incompetent persons may sue through a representative, next friend, or guardian ad litem, and in real property actions unknown claimants or unknown heirs may be named and described as such after diligent inquiry.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"18","t":"Joinder of claims and remedies","s":"A party may join as many claims, legal or equitable, as it has against an opposing party. Claims that once had to await the outcome of another claim may be joined in one action, so a plaintiff may seek money and to set aside a fraudulent conveyance without first getting a money judgment.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"19","t":"Required joinder of parties","s":"A person who can be served and whose joinder will not destroy subject matter jurisdiction must be joined if complete relief is impossible without the person or if the person’s absence would impair the person’s interest or expose existing parties to inconsistent obligations; a joined party who validly objects to venue is dismissed. If joinder is not feasible, the court decides in equity and good conscience whether to proceed or dismiss, weighing prejudice, ways to lessen it, adequacy of a judgment, and whether the plaintiff has another remedy. Pleadings must name required persons not joined and explain why, subject to Rule 23.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"20","t":"Permissive joinder of parties","s":"Plaintiffs may join, and defendants may be joined, in one action if the claims arise from the same transaction, occurrence, or series and share a common question of law or fact. Not every party needs an interest in all the relief, and judgment may be given for or against parties according to their rights and liabilities. The court may order separate trials or other protective orders to prevent delay, expense, or prejudice.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"21","t":"Misjoinder and nonjoinder of parties","s":"Misjoinder of parties is not a ground for dismissal. The court may add or drop parties at any stage on motion or on its own, on just terms, and may sever any claim against a party.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"22","t":"Interpleader","s":"A plaintiff exposed to double or multiple liability may join the competing claimants as defendants and require them to interplead, even if their claims do not share a common origin or the plaintiff denies liability. A defendant facing similar exposure may seek interpleader by cross-claim or counterclaim, and this rule supplements Rule 20 joinder.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"23","t":"Class actions","s":"One or more members may sue or be sued for a class only if the class is too numerous for joinder, there are common questions, the representatives’ claims are typical, and they will adequately protect the class, plus one of three conditions: risk of inconsistent or impairing separate judgments, conduct generally applicable to the class warranting injunctive or declaratory relief, or predominance of common questions and superiority of a class action. The court decides certification by order as soon as practicable, and in predominance (b)(3) classes must give the best practicable notice, including individual notice to identifiable members, with the right to opt out by a set date. The court may manage the case by orders, issue classes or subclasses, and no class action may be dismissed or settled without court approval and notice to the class.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"23.1","t":"Derivative actions by shareholders","s":"A holder of shares, voting trust certificates, or a beneficial interest may sue on a corporation’s behalf through a verified complaint alleging ownership at the time of the wrong, though the court may allow a non-qualifying shareholder to proceed after a hearing on a strong prima facie case and other listed factors. The plaintiff must first make a formal demand on the board unless excused because a majority of directors is implicated (excuse must be pleaded with particularity), a board’s business-judgment refusal ends the claim, and disinterested directors may petition to dismiss subject to the court’s independent appraisal. If plaintiffs hold less than five percent of a class of shares, defendants may demand security for expenses including attorney fees; settlement or dismissal requires court approval, and a successful plaintiff may recover reasonable expenses and attorney fees.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"23.2","t":"Actions relating to unincorporated associations","s":"An action by or against members of an unincorporated association as a class, through named representative members, may proceed only if the representatives will fairly and adequately protect the association and its members. The court may issue management orders like those in Rule 23(d), and dismissal or compromise follows Rule 23(e).","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"24","t":"Intervention","s":"On timely application, a person must be allowed to intervene if the person claims an interest in the subject of the action that may be impaired and is not adequately represented, and may be allowed to intervene when the person’s claim or defense shares a common question of law or fact, or when a government officer or agency administers a statute or order a party relies on. The applicant must serve a motion stating the grounds with a pleading setting out the claim or defense, and the court considers undue delay or prejudice. When the constitutionality of a state statute affecting the public interest is challenged in a case without the State, the court must notify the Alaska Attorney General, and the State may intervene.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"25","t":"Substitution of parties","s":"If a party dies and the claim survives, the successors, representatives, or any party may move to substitute, serving parties under Rule 5 and nonparties under Rule 4, and the action is dismissed as to the deceased party unless the motion is made within 90 days after the death is suggested on the record. The court may continue the case by or against the representative of a party who becomes incompetent, or substitute or join a transferee of an interest. A public officer sued officially who leaves office is automatically replaced by the successor, and may be named by official title.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"26","t":"General discovery provisions and duty of disclosure","s":"Except in Rule 16(g)-exempt cases, adoptions, and prisoner suits against the State under AS 09.19, each party must, without a request, disclose the factual basis of its claims or defenses, witnesses with relevant information, recorded statements, relevant documents and photos, insurance agreements, damages computations, and (an Alaska addition) potentially responsible persons under AS 09.17.080 against whom it will seek to allocate fault, within 10 days after the parties’ planning meeting, which must be held at least 14 days before a scheduling conference or order and followed within 10 days by a written discovery plan. Retained experts must give a signed report listing opinions, data considered, qualifications with publications from the preceding 10 years, compensation, and testimony from the preceding 4 years; no more than 3 independent experts per side may testify on the same issue absent good cause, and objections to pretrial disclosures must be filed within 14 days or are generally waived. Discovery covers any nonprivileged matter relevant to the subject matter of the action and reasonably calculated to lead to admissible evidence (broader than the current federal proportionality standard), subject to court limits, protections for trial preparation materials and non-testifying experts, privilege logs, and protective orders after a good-faith conference. Before the meeting a party may serve only up to 10 of its 30 allowed interrogatories, duties to supplement apply, and in personal injury or property damage cases with less than $100,000 in claims (filed on or after August 7, 1997), discovery is limited to that allowed in District Court Civil Rule 1(a)(1) with expedited calendaring under District Court Civil Rule 4.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"26.1","t":"Discovery and disclosure in divorce and legal separation actions","s":"In divorce and legal separation cases, each party must, without a request and within 45 days after the answer is filed, disclose real property interests with appraisals and assessments from the last 2 years; signed releases valid for 6 months for employer earnings and benefits, retirement plans, and financial accounts; all accounts used in the past 2 years with 3 months of statements; debts; personal property worth over $100; investment statements; federal tax returns for the past 3 years; pay stubs for the past 2 months; claimed non-marital property with reasons; medical coverage details; and anything required by local order. These disclosures are a minimum and do not limit other discovery, which may proceed on the timing of Rule 26(d)(2). This Alaska-specific rule has no federal counterpart.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"27","t":"Depositions before action or pending appeal","s":"A person who expects to be a party to an Alaska action but cannot yet bring it may file a verified petition in superior court to perpetuate testimony, describing the expected case, the facts to be proved, expected adverse parties, and witnesses, and must serve notice at least 20 days before the hearing under Rule 4(d) (or 4(h) mail service), with court-appointed counsel for unserved adverse parties. If perpetuation may prevent a failure or delay of justice, the court orders the depositions, which may later be used under Rule 32(a). While an appeal or petition for review is pending, or before the time to file one expires, the trial court may also allow depositions to perpetuate testimony on motion.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"28","t":"Persons before whom depositions may be taken","s":"In Alaska, depositions are taken before an officer authorized to administer oaths or a court-appointed person; outside Alaska, before a person authorized locally or by U.S. law, a court-commissioned person, or under a letter rogatory, and foreign letter-rogatory evidence is not excluded merely for being non-verbatim or unsworn. A deposition may not be taken before a relative, employee, or attorney of a party or of a party’s counsel, or anyone financially interested in the action, except that an attorney in the case may operate the recording equipment for an audio or audio-visual deposition.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"29","t":"Stipulations about discovery practice","s":"Unless the court directs otherwise, parties may agree in writing to take depositions before any person, at any time or place, on any notice and in any manner, and to modify other discovery procedures and limits. However, stipulations extending response times under Rules 33, 34, and 36 need court approval if they would interfere with deadlines set for motions, hearings, or trial.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"30","t":"Depositions upon oral examination","s":"Any person may be deposed without leave, but leave is required for a prisoner, for a deponent already deposed, for a deposition before the Rule 26(d) time (unless the deponent is certified to be leaving Alaska), or if without stipulation it would exceed 3 depositions per side (plaintiffs, defendants, or third-party defendants) of witnesses other than parties, independent trial experts, treating physicians, and necessary document custodians—an Alaska limit stricter than the federal 10. Without stipulation or order, depositions may not exceed 6 hours for parties, independent experts, and treating physicians, and 3 hours for other deponents, compared to the single 7-hour federal limit; objections must be concise and nonsuggestive, and instructions not to answer are allowed only to preserve privilege, enforce a court limit, or move to terminate. The notice must give reasonable written notice of time, place, and deponent, organizations named as deponents must designate witnesses, and depositions may be taken by phone or remote means by stipulation or order. On request before the deposition ends, the deponent gets 30 days after notice of availability to review and sign changes; custodians of original depositions must keep them for 1 year after the appeal time expires or the appeal concludes, and a noticing party who fails to attend or subpoena a witness may have to pay others’ expenses and attorney’s fees.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"30.1","t":"Audio and audio-visual depositions","s":"Any oral deposition may be recorded by audio or audio-visual means without a stenographer, and the recording is an official record usable like a stenographic deposition, though the court may order a transcript for good cause and any party may make its own simultaneous record at its own expense. The notice and subpoena must say the deposition will be so recorded and whether a court reporter will be absent. The recording must open with a statement of the operator, date, place, caption, witness, and stipulations, administer the oath on the record, show the witness waist-up, and announce each unit change and the conclusion; the noticing party keeps the original, and reasonable recording costs may be taxed as costs.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"31","t":"Depositions upon written questions","s":"Any person may be deposed on written questions, subject to the same leave requirements and per-side limit of 3 nonparty, non-expert depositions as Rule 30, with the notice naming the deponent and officer. Cross questions may be served within 30 days after the notice and questions, redirect questions within 10 days after cross questions, and recross questions within 10 days after redirect, unless the court changes the times. The officer takes the answers and prepares and certifies the deposition, and the party taking it gives prompt notice of filing.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"32","t":"Use of depositions in court proceedings","s":"At trial or a hearing, admissible parts of a deposition may be used against a party who attended or had reasonable notice: any deposition to impeach or as the evidence rules allow, a party’s or its officer’s or designee’s deposition by an adverse party for any purpose, and any witness’s deposition if the witness is dead, more than 100 miles away or out of state, unable to attend, not subpoenable, in exceptional circumstances, or (an Alaska addition) the testimony was recorded on video tape. A deposition may not be used against a party who could not get counsel for an early deposition noticed under Rule 30(a)(2)(C), or who received less than 11 days’ notice and promptly moved for a protective order still pending. Errors in the notice, officer disqualification, form of questions, and transcription are waived unless objected to promptly, and objections to Rule 31 written questions must be served within the time for the next round and within 5 days after the last questions.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"33","t":"Interrogatories to parties","s":"Without leave or written stipulation, a party may serve no more than 30 interrogatories, including discrete subparts, on another party (versus 25 under the federal rule), counting up to 10 served early under Rule 26(d)(1), and must leave space for the answers. Answers must be made separately and fully under oath and served within 30 days, any objection must be specific or it is waived absent good cause, and contention interrogatories are allowed. A party may answer by specifying business records, including electronically stored information, when the burden of finding the answer is substantially the same for both sides.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"34","t":"Production of documents, ESI, and things; entry on land","s":"A party may request another party to produce for inspection, copying, testing, or sampling documents, electronically stored information, and tangible things in its possession, custody, or control, or to permit entry onto land, describing items with reasonable particularity and possibly specifying the ESI form; requests may not be served before the Rule 26(d) time without leave or stipulation. The responding party must serve a written response within 30 days, stating for each item that inspection will be allowed or the reasons for any objection, and stating the ESI form it will use if it objects to the requested form or none was specified. Documents are produced as kept in the usual course of business or labeled by request category, ESI in its ordinary or a reasonably usable form and only in one form, and the rule does not bar an independent action against a nonparty.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"35","t":"Physical and mental examinations","s":"When a party’s physical or mental condition (including blood group), or that of a person in its custody or legal control, is in controversy, the court may order an examination by a suitably licensed or certified examiner, on motion for good cause and notice, specifying the time, place, manner, conditions, scope, and examiner. On request, the party who caused the exam must provide the examiner’s detailed written report and earlier reports on the same condition, and is then entitled to similar reports from the examined party; an examiner who refuses to report may be barred from testifying. By requesting the report or deposing the examiner, the examined party waives privilege as to other examiners of the same condition, and these provisions also apply to agreed examinations.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"36","t":"Requests for admission","s":"A party may serve written requests to admit facts, opinions of fact, application of law to fact, or genuineness of documents (with copies attached), but not before the Rule 26(d) time without leave or stipulation; each matter is admitted unless a signed written answer or objection is served within 30 days. Denials must fairly meet the substance, lack of knowledge is allowed only after reasonable inquiry, and a party may not object merely because the matter is a genuine issue for trial; the requesting party may move to test the sufficiency of answers, and the court may deem matters admitted. Admissions are conclusive for the pending action only unless the court allows withdrawal or amendment. Rule 36 itself states no numerical limit on requests, although Rule 26(b)(2)(A) refers to the court altering “the number of requests under Rule 36,” so any default cap is unclear from the text.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"37","t":"Failure to disclose or cooperate in discovery; sanctions","s":"A party may move to compel Rule 26(a) or Rule 26.1(b) disclosures or discovery responses after certifying a good-faith effort to confer, evasive or incomplete answers count as failures, and the losing side (or its attorney) must generally pay the winner’s reasonable expenses and attorney’s fees unless substantially justified. For disobeying a discovery order, the court may deem facts established, bar evidence, strike pleadings, dismiss, enter default, or hold a party in contempt, and must award expenses; but before imposing those severe sanctions it must consider willfulness, materiality, prejudice, the link between the violation and the sanction, and lesser sanctions, and may not effectively decide a claim, defense, or central issue unless it finds willful conduct—an Alaska standard not in the federal rule. A party that fails without substantial justification to make required Rule 26(a), 26(e)(1), or 26.1(b) disclosures may not use the undisclosed witness or information unless harmless, the jury may be told of the failure, and a party who unreasonably refuses a Rule 36 admission later proven may have to pay the cost of proof. Sanctions also apply for failing to attend one’s own deposition or respond to interrogatories or inspection requests, no sanctions apply to ESI lost through routine good-faith system operation absent exceptional circumstances, and abusive discovery conduct or failure to help frame the Rule 26(f) plan can bring an award of expenses and attorney’s fees.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"38","t":"Jury trial","s":"Preserves the right to a jury trial under article I, section 16 of the Alaska Constitution or a state statute. A party demands a jury by serving a separate signed written demand any time after the case starts and no later than 10 days after service of the last pleading on the issue; if the demand covers only some issues, any other party may demand a jury on other issues within 10 days after service (or less if the court orders). Failing to serve and file a demand waives the jury, and a demand can be withdrawn only with the parties’ consent, which may be implied by failing to appear at trial.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"39","t":"Trial by jury or by the court","s":"Issues for which a jury was properly demanded are tried to a jury unless the parties stipulate in writing or in open court to a bench trial, or the court finds no jury right exists. Issues not demanded are tried by the judge, though the court may still order a jury on motion. In cases with no jury right, the court may use an advisory jury or, with both parties’ consent, a jury whose verdict is binding.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"40","t":"Assignment of cases, calendars, and continuances","s":"The clerk keeps a master calendar, and a case is set for a pretrial or trial-setting conference or trial once it is at issue and a party files a memorandum to set the case for trial listing required information; a party who disagrees must file its own memorandum within 10 days after service. Orders are sought from the assigned judge, with exceptions for emergencies, uncontested matters, and similar situations. A trial continuance generally must be requested at least 5 days before trial with a supporting affidavit, and the court may impose terms such as jury fees and costs; an editor’s note says the calendar-precedence provision in (e)(3) is suspended for the Anchorage trial courts.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"41","t":"Dismissal of actions","s":"A plaintiff may dismiss without a court order by notice before the other side serves an answer or summary judgment motion, or by stipulation of all parties who have appeared; this is without prejudice unless stated, but a second notice dismissal of the same claim (after a prior dismissal in any state or federal court) operates as a judgment on the merits. Otherwise dismissal requires a court order, and a defendant may move for involuntary dismissal for failure to prosecute or obey rules or orders, which counts as a merits adjudication unless the order says otherwise (except for jurisdiction, venue, or Rule 19 joinder). The court may dismiss for want of prosecution a case pending more than one year with no proceedings, or more than one year with no trial or mandatory pretrial scheduling conference set or held; the clerk reviews cases twice a year, and if good cause isn’t shown at a calendar call or within 60 days after notice, the case is dismissed without prejudice unless the order says otherwise. Former paragraph (a)(3) was repealed by statute effective May 11, 2017.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"42","t":"Consolidation, separate trials, and change of judge","s":"The court may consolidate cases sharing common questions (the motion is filed in the court where consolidation is sought, listing every case, with notice filed in all affected courts) and may order separate trials of claims or issues while preserving the jury right. Under 42(c), each side in superior or district court gets one peremptory change of judge and one of a master as a matter of right; parties on the same side count as one side, though the presiding judge may allow an extra change for a party whose interests are adverse to a co-party. The “Notice of Change of Judge” must name the judge, state no grounds, and include no affidavit, and it is timely only if filed before trial begins and within 5 days after notice that the case was assigned to a specific judge (or, for a party joining later, within 5 days after that party appears or files a pleading); a for-cause disqualification motion filed within that window tolls the time for all parties, and a new 5-day period runs from notice of its denial. The right is waived by knowingly participating before a permanently assigned judge in a merits proceeding involving evidence or affidavits, a pretrial conference, or the start of trial, or by agreeing to that judge; the presiding judge then reassigns within the district (seeking a Chief Justice assignment order if all judges are disqualified), and if an assigned judge later becomes unavailable, the parties’ rights are restored.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"43","t":"Evidence","s":"Rescinded by Supreme Court Order 366, effective August 1, 1979.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"43.1","t":"Exhibits","s":"Parties must mark all intended exhibits before trial and file an original and one copy of an exhibit list with the in-court clerk at the start of trial, using procedures set by the administrative director’s bulletin (Administrative Bulletin No. 9, reproduced after the rule). Offered exhibits go into the clerk’s custody, and before the case is submitted counsel and self-represented parties must check the exhibits against the clerk’s list. Admitted exhibits go to the jury except live ammunition, firearms, drugs and alcohol, perishable or hazardous materials, and money or valuables, which need a specific order (a photo may be substituted); after trial the court may have counsel keep such items, plus bulky ones, pending appeal and may require an affidavit about safekeeping.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"44","t":"Proof of official record","s":"Rescinded by Supreme Court Order 366, effective August 1, 1979.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"44.1","t":"Determination of foreign law","s":"Rescinded by Supreme Court Order 366, effective August 1, 1979.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"45","t":"Subpoena","s":"The clerk issues subpoenas under the court’s seal, including blank signed witness subpoenas for parties to fill in, and a subpoena may require production of documents, electronically stored information, or things; the court may quash or modify an unreasonable and oppressive one on a motion made at or before the compliance time, or require advance payment of production costs. Any nonparty at least 18 years old or a peace officer may serve by delivering a copy and tendering one day’s attendance fees and mileage (not required for the state or local governments), or the clerk may serve by registered or certified mail with the fees enclosed. For depositions, a person may serve written objections to inspection within 10 days after service (or by the compliance time if sooner), after which inspection requires a court order; residents can be required to attend anywhere in the judicial district, trial subpoenas may be served anywhere in the state, and disobedience without adequate excuse may be contempt.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"45.1","t":"Interstate depositions and discovery","s":"Adopted from the Uniform Interstate Depositions and Discovery Act, this rule lets a party in an out-of-state case submit a foreign subpoena to any Alaska clerk of court, who promptly issues an Alaska subpoena incorporating its terms and listing contact information for all counsel and unrepresented parties; doing so is not an appearance in Alaska courts. The subpoena is served under Civil Rule 45(c), Alaska’s discovery rules (Civil Rules 26 to 37) apply, and motions to quash, modify, enforce, or for protection go to the Alaska court location that issued it.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"46","t":"Conduct of trials","s":"Sets the order of trial: the plaintiff states the claim, the defendant states the defense, the plaintiff then the defendant present evidence, followed by rebuttal only unless the court allows more. Unless the court orders otherwise, only one attorney per side may examine a given witness, a lawyer who testifies on the merits for a client may not argue to the jury without permission, and formal exceptions are unnecessary if a party makes its objection known. The plaintiff opens and closes argument, no more than two counsel per party may address the jury unless allowed, a plaintiff who waives opening argument cannot reply, the court may set time limits, and courtroom photography or broadcasting is allowed only under Administrative Rule 50.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"47","t":"Jurors","s":"The court or the attorneys may conduct jury selection, and the court may impose reasonable time limits. The court may seat one or two alternates, either designated alternates or extra jurors with excess jurors removed at random before deliberations, and each party gets one extra peremptory challenge when alternates are used. The rule lists 14 grounds for challenge for cause (including bias, relationship within the fourth degree, certain business or family relationships, being accused by the challenging party within the previous two years, and financial interest), and each party gets three peremptory challenges, with co-parties on one side treated as one party unless they have adverse interests and different attorneys. Parties may stipulate to fewer than twelve jurors or a majority verdict, and the rule sets out the jurors’ oath.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"48","t":"Order of trial and management of juries","s":"After argument, the court charges the jury in writing, reads the instructions, and the jury takes the written charge to the jury room. The court may order a jury view of property, must admonish jurors who separate not to discuss the case, may discharge a juror who cannot continue (proceeding with the rest by consent, swearing a new juror and restarting, or forming a new jury), and keeps deliberating jurors in an officer’s charge with no outside communication. The jury takes admitted exhibits except depositions, may be discharged before verdict only by consent of all parties or when agreement appears improbable, and a case may be retried if the jury is discharged without a verdict.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"49","t":"Verdicts, special verdicts, and interrogatories","s":"The foreperson delivers a written, signed verdict in court; the court may allow a sealed after-hours verdict, and any party may have the jury polled, with further deliberation if agreement is lacking. The court may require a special verdict with written findings on each fact issue (an omitted issue is waived unless a party demands its submission before the jury retires) or a general verdict with written interrogatories, and the rule explains what happens when answers and verdict conflict. Statutory notes reproduced with the rule (AS 09.17.040 and 09.17.080) require itemized personal-injury damages and allocation of fault percentages, and limit a party under 50 percent at fault to joint liability of no more than twice its percentage.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"50","t":"Directed verdict and judgment notwithstanding the verdict","s":"A motion for directed verdict must state specific grounds, does not waive the right to present evidence if denied, and does not waive the jury even if all parties move. A party who moved for a directed verdict at the close of all evidence may move for judgment notwithstanding the verdict no later than 10 days after the date in the clerk’s certificate of distribution on the judgment (or within 10 days after the jury is discharged if no verdict was returned), and may join or alternatively request a new trial. If JNOV is granted, the court must also conditionally rule on any new-trial motion, and the losing party may move for a new trial under Rule 59 within 10 days after entry of the JNOV judgment.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"51","t":"Instructions to jury","s":"Any party may file written instruction requests at the close of evidence or earlier as directed, and the court must tell counsel the final instructions before closing arguments and may instruct before or after arguments and at any stage of trial. No party may claim error in instructions unless it objected before the jury retired, stating the matter and grounds, and the court must allow objections outside the jury’s hearing. The court must tell jurors they are the exclusive judges of the facts and instruct them on all necessary law.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"52","t":"Findings by the court","s":"In bench trials and with advisory juries, and when granting or refusing interlocutory injunctions, the court must state findings of fact and conclusions of law, which are set aside on review only if clearly erroneous; findings are not needed for decisions on Rule 12 or 56 motions or other motions except under Rule 41(b). A motion to amend or add findings must be made no later than 10 days after the date in the clerk’s certificate of distribution on the judgment and may be joined with a Rule 59 motion. Preparation of proposed findings is governed by Rule 78.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"53","t":"Masters","s":"The presiding judge, with the chief justice’s approval, may appoint standing masters, and courts may appoint special masters (including referees, auditors, examiners, and magistrate judges), with compensation set by the court. Unless the order says otherwise, the master sets the first meeting within 20 days after the order of reference, may take evidence and swear witnesses, and files a report with notice mailed to all parties. In nonjury cases, objections are due within 10 days after notice of the filing; with no objections the court may accept the findings without independent review, but if objections are filed the court must review the relevant transcript or recording and decide factual objections de novo, unless the parties stipulate (with the court’s consent) to clear-error review or final findings. In jury cases the master’s findings are admissible evidence.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"54","t":"Judgments and costs","s":"A judgment includes a decree, may not recite pleadings or prior proceedings, and money judgments must follow Rule 58.2, with proposed judgments governed by Rule 78. In multi-claim or multi-party cases, the court may enter final judgment on fewer than all claims or parties only on an express finding of no just reason for delay and an express direction for entry. A default judgment cannot differ in kind from or exceed the demand, other judgments grant the relief the party is entitled to, and costs go to the prevailing party as of course unless a statute, rule, or the court provides otherwise, with taxation of costs by the clerk under Rule 79.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"55","t":"Default","s":"A party seeking default must serve the application on all parties, including the party in default, and the clerk may enter default no sooner than 7 days after service of the application. The clerk may enter a default judgment for a sum certain against a defendant who failed to appear, on an affidavit of the amount due and of the defendant’s non-minor, competent, and military status, but not in a multi-defendant case unless all are defaulted; otherwise the court decides, and a party who has appeared must get written notice at least 3 days before the decision (unless that party fails to appear for trial). Default may be set aside for good cause and a default judgment under Rule 60(b); no default judgment may be entered against the state without satisfactory evidence. A party entitled to default judgment without a hearing must include an itemized cost statement and actual attorney’s fees in the application (with fees governed by Rule 82(b)(4)) and a proposed judgment in Rule 58.2 form.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"56","t":"Summary judgment","s":"A claimant may move any time after 20 days from the start of the action (or after the opponent moves), and a defending party may move at any time, following Rule 77 with a memorandum showing no genuine issue of material fact. The opponent may file affidavits and a concise “statement of genuine issues,” may not rest on its pleadings, and may get a continuance for discovery if it shows by affidavit why it cannot yet present the facts; judgment may be entered against the moving party when appropriate. A decision granting summary judgment is not itself a final judgment under Rule 58; when it resolves all claims as to all parties, the proposed final judgment must be filed within 20 days of service of the decision as a separate document. The court may specify facts established without substantial controversy, and bad-faith affidavits lead to expenses, attorney’s fees, and possible contempt.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"57","t":"Declaratory judgments and confession of judgment","s":"Declaratory judgment actions follow these rules, including jury demands under Rules 38 and 39; another adequate remedy does not bar declaratory relief, and the court may expedite the hearing. A defendant may confess judgment in a pending case for no more than, and nothing different from, what the complaint demands, by a signed written confession with the plaintiff’s written assent, acknowledged before an oath-giving officer unless the parties appear in court. Confession of judgment without a lawsuit is also allowed for money due or to become due or to secure against contingent liability, but the confession must be verified under oath, state a particular sum, and explain the underlying facts.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"58","t":"Entry of judgment","s":"Subject to Rule 54(b), the court or clerk enters judgment promptly on a general verdict or a decision for a sum certain, costs, or denial of all relief, and the court promptly enters judgment on other decisions and special verdicts. Every judgment must be a separate document distinct from findings, opinions, or memoranda, and entry is not delayed, nor the appeal time extended, for taxing costs or awarding fees. Money judgments must follow Rule 58.2; statutory notes add that 50 percent of punitive damages (for causes of action accruing on or after August 7, 1997) goes to the state general fund and that no prejudgment interest is allowed on future damages or punitive damages.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"58.1","t":"Effective dates and start of time for appeal and reconsideration","s":"Orders and judgments take effect when entered: an oral order when the judge puts it on the official electronic record (unless the judge specifies otherwise), a written order or judgment when signed unless it states otherwise, and all judgments must be in writing. Time to appeal or seek reconsideration or review runs from the date of notice, which for written orders and judgments is the date in the clerk’s certificate of distribution, and for oral orders is the announcement date for parties present (or the distribution date of notice for those absent, or of the written order if the judge says one will follow). Every written order, judgment, and notice of oral order must carry a clerk’s certificate of distribution showing the date, recipients, and the distributing employee.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"58.2","t":"Judgments for the payment of money","s":"A money judgment must identify each creditor and debtor (with full legal names and dates of birth if known) and include, as applicable, the principal, the portion bearing prejudgment interest with rate and start date, blanks for the court to fill in prejudgment interest, attorney’s fees, costs, and the total, punitive damages, the post-judgment interest rate, and any State of Alaska interest in punitive damages, following the sample forms. When multiple rates or dates apply, the preparing party submits a separate interest computation sheet instead. The judge’s name must be typed under the signature line, child support orders are excluded (Rule 90.3(j) governs), and the clerk may reject noncompliant proposed judgments; the sample punitive-damages form shows 50% of the award going to the state.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"59","t":"New trials and amendment of judgments","s":"The court may grant a new trial on all or some issues in jury or bench cases if required in the interest of justice. A new-trial motion must be served no later than 10 days after the date in the clerk’s certificate of distribution on the judgment, opposing affidavits are due within 10 days after service (extendable up to 20 more days by court or stipulation), and newly discovered evidence must be supported by specified affidavits or documents; the court may order a new trial on its own within 10 days after entry of judgment. A motion to alter or amend a judgment must be served no later than 10 days after entry of judgment, which is a shorter window than the federal rule.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"60","t":"Relief from judgment or order","s":"Clerical mistakes, including in electronic case records, may be corrected at any time, but during an appeal only before the record is filed in the Supreme Court or later with its leave. The court may relieve a party from a final judgment or order for mistake or excusable neglect, newly discovered evidence, fraud or misconduct, a void judgment, a satisfied or no-longer-equitable judgment, or any other justifying reason; the motion must be made within a reasonable time, and for the first three grounds no more than one year after the date of notice of the judgment or order under Rule 58.1(c) (rather than from entry, as the federal rule measures it). The motion does not suspend the judgment, independent actions remain available, old writs such as coram nobis are abolished, and a motion to amend or correct a judgment must include a proposed amended or corrected judgment and separate proposed order.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"61","t":"Harmless error","s":"No error in admitting or excluding evidence, or in any ruling or act by the court or parties, justifies a new trial or disturbing a judgment unless refusing to act would be inconsistent with substantial justice. The court must disregard errors that do not affect the parties’ substantial rights.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"62","t":"Stay of proceedings to enforce a judgment","s":"Except for default, consent, or confession judgments, no execution or enforcement may occur until 10 days after the date in the clerk’s certificate of distribution on the judgment, but injunction and receivership judgments are not automatically stayed. The court may stay enforcement pending motions under Rules 50, 52(b), 59, or 60, and may suspend, modify, or grant an injunction pending appeal on terms securing the other side. An appellant may obtain a stay by a court-approved supersedeas bond, the state and its agencies need no bond, the Supreme Court’s stay powers are preserved, and the court may stay a partial Rule 54(b) judgment until later judgments are entered.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"63","t":"Disability of a judge","s":"If a judge dies, becomes ill, or is otherwise disabled before trial, another judge assigned by the presiding judge or the chief justice may take over. During trial, a substitute judge may continue as if present from the start, provided a stenographic or electronic record has been made so the new judge can become familiar with the proceedings. After a verdict or findings, a substitute judge may complete the case but may grant a new trial if unable to perform the duties for not having presided or another reason.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"64","t":"Seizure of person or property","s":"At the start of and during a case, remedies for seizing a person or property to secure satisfaction of the eventual judgment, such as arrest, attachment, garnishment, replevin, and sequestration, are available as provided by the law in effect when the remedy is sought. A statutory note says AS 34.40.113(f) bars attachment or similar relief in certain cases involving discretionary interests in irrevocable trusts.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"65","t":"Injunctions","s":"No preliminary injunction issues without notice, and the court may consolidate the preliminary injunction hearing with trial on the merits. A temporary restraining order without notice requires specific facts by affidavit or verified complaint showing immediate irreparable harm and an attorney certification about notice efforts; it expires within a time the court sets, not exceeding 10 days, unless extended for good cause for a like period or by consent, and the adverse party may move to dissolve it on 2 days’ notice or less. Security is required except from the state or a municipality, or, unless ordered, in domestic relations cases, and every injunction must state reasons and specifically describe the restrained acts. Presiding judges may issue standing injunctions in domestic relations cases (excluding dissolutions, domestic violence, and reciprocal enforcement actions) barring removal of children from Alaska, disposing of marital property without written consent, and threats or harassment, effective on receipt by the party or attorney.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"65.1","t":"Protective orders: confidential petitioner contact information","s":"A self-represented petitioner seeking a domestic violence protective order (AS 18.66.100 or 18.66.110) or a stalking or sexual assault protective order (AS 18.65.850 or 18.65.855) may put a mailing address and phone number on a separate form and leave them off other filings. Access is limited to the court, authorized court personnel, and the petitioner, except that it may be shared with the Child Support Services Division if a child support order is entered, which may not disclose it further; a similar limit applies to an information sheet for law enforcement that the court keeps.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"66","t":"Receivers","s":"A case in which a receiver has been appointed may be dismissed only by court order. Administration of estates by receivers follows the statutes, and otherwise receivership cases are governed by law and these rules.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"67","t":"Deposit in court","s":"With notice to all parties and court permission, a party may deposit money or other deliverable property that is the subject of the case or due under a judgment, managed under Administrative Rule 5. The court releases it to the party entitled to it, and no interest accrues against the depositing party on the deposited amount after deposit.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"68 (older cases)","t":"Offer of judgment (cases filed before August 7, 1997)","s":"Any time more than 10 days before trial, a claimant or defending party may serve an offer to allow judgment on stated terms with costs then accrued; it cannot be revoked for 10 days, is accepted by written notice within 10 days (after which the clerk enters judgment), and is otherwise deemed withdrawn and inadmissible except on costs. If the final judgment is not more favorable to the offeree than the offer, prejudgment interest is adjusted: a claimant-offeree’s interest rate is reduced by the amount in AS 09.30.065 and the claimant must pay the offeror’s post-offer costs and attorney’s fees (calculated under Rules 79 and 82) and cannot recover its own; a defendant-offeree’s interest rate is increased by the AS 09.30.065 amount. A party found liable may make an offer with the same effect within a reasonable time not less than 10 days before hearings on the amount of damages.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"68","t":"Offer of judgment","s":"Any time more than 10 days before trial, either side may serve an offer to allow judgment on stated terms with costs then accrued; it cannot be revoked for 10 days, is accepted by written notice within 10 days, after which the clerk enters judgment, and is otherwise deemed withdrawn and inadmissible except on costs, though new offers may follow. If the final judgment is at least 5 percent less favorable to the offeree than the offer (or at least 10 percent with multiple defendants), the offeree, whether claimant or defendant, pays all allowable costs plus a share of the offeror’s reasonable actual attorney’s fees incurred from the offer date: 75 percent if the offer was served no later than 60 days after the pretrial-order date for Rule 26 initial disclosures; 50 percent if served more than 60 days after that date but more than 90 days before trial; and 30 percent if served 90 days or less but more than 10 days before trial. The offeror is treated as the prevailing party under Rule 82 and receives the larger of the Rule 82 award or the Rule 68 amount, but not both; this fee-shifting, which applies to either side, goes well beyond the federal rule’s cost-only shifting against plaintiffs.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"69","t":"Execution and examination of judgment debtor","s":"Judgments are enforced by writ of execution unless the court directs otherwise, and creditors may take discovery in aid of judgment; the court may order the debtor to appear and answer under oath about property, order property applied to the judgment, and bar its transfer, with contempt for disobedience. If five years pass with no writ outstanding, a writ issues only on a motion and affidavit showing just and sufficient reasons, served on the debtor by summons under Rule 4, with the debtor’s response due within 20 days. Generally only one general writ and one earnings-garnishment writ may be outstanding at once (with exceptions for Permanent Fund Dividend levies and property in another community), process servers must deliver seized money to the court the next business day and return the writ within 30 days after notice of termination, and Rule 4(c)(3) servers or peace officers serve writs (the clerk may serve PFD writs by certified mail). The creditor must serve statutory notices on the debtor (for AS 09.38.080 levies, within 3 days after levy), need not re-serve for a later levy described in an affidavit served within the past 45 days, and must file proof of service or a diligent-inquiry request within 30 days after the court receives seized money or the money may be returned to the debtor; a real property sale is confirmed on motion after 10 days from the return of sale unless the debtor objects within 10 days, and the rule also covers PFD executions, government bank sweeps, and non-attorney representation of municipalities.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"70","t":"Judgment for specific acts; vesting title","s":"If a party fails to perform a court-ordered act such as conveying land or delivering documents within the time set, the court may have another appointed person do it at that party’s expense with the same effect, and the clerk will issue a writ of attachment or sequestration on request; contempt is also available. For property in Alaska, the court may instead enter a judgment transferring title, which acts as a conveyance, and a party awarded possession is entitled to a writ of execution or assistance.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"71","t":"Process for and against nonparties","s":"A nonparty in whose favor an order is made may enforce it with the same process as a party. A nonparty against whom an order may lawfully be enforced is subject to the same enforcement process as a party.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"72","t":"Eminent domain","s":"Condemnation cases follow the Civil Rules except as this rule provides: the complaint (with a case description form, and a declaration of taking if used) must state the authority, necessity, use, property, interests taken, estimated just compensation (a judicial admission), and known claimants, with the decisional document and a map attached. A defendant who contests authority and necessity or the declaration of taking must answer within twenty days after service, and one who disputes compensation or wants notice must file a notice of appearance within twenty days; no counterclaims, cross-claims, or third-party claims are allowed unless consolidated under Rule 42(a), and default judgment may be entered against nonappearing defendants ten days after the final compensation determination. Discovery responses on authority, necessity, and possession are due within fifteen days (but not earlier than ten days after the answer deadline); appraisals from the prior five years are exchanged within forty-five days after filing, and new appraisals and expert reports forty-five days before the master’s hearing and thirty days before discovery closes; objections to a declaration of taking require a motion to dismiss within thirty days after service, with opposition in ten days, reply in three days, and a hearing within twenty days of a request; other authority motions may be filed no earlier than sixty days after service on all defendants. Compensation is set by a court-appointed master unless all parties waive (Rule 53 does not apply), with appeal by trial de novo within ten days of service of the report for the plaintiff and fifteen days for a defendant and a jury demand within twenty days; the plaintiff pays the defendant’s costs and fees if the taking is denied, the plaintiff alone appeals the master’s award, the award is at least ten (10) percent above the deposit or appealed master’s allowance, the case is dismissed, or justice requires, and Rule 68 offers apply with special limits (a defendant never pays the plaintiff’s costs and fees, and want-of-prosecution dismissals are barred).","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"72.1","t":"Expert advisory panels in health care malpractice cases","s":"In a malpractice action subject to AS 09.55.536, either party may ask the court to appoint an expert advisory panel; once the case is at issue the court nominates three members, parties may move to disqualify within 10 days after notice (opposition within five days, no reply), and nominees must disclose conflicts within 10 days. Within 30 days after the initial nominations, the plaintiff and each provider defendant serve copies of all discoverable medical records, and each party serves its Rule 26(a)(1)(A)-(C) and 26(a)(2) disclosures; a defendant has 30 days to answer a plaintiff’s consultant reports with its own. The court gives the panel written instructions (including the AS 09.55.536 questions and separate opinions for each provider), attorneys may attend but not question at panel interviews, and the panel may not otherwise communicate with the parties. Without leave of court, discovery waits until the panel report is filed or, for cases filed on or after August 7, 1997, 60 days after panel selection (80 days after the case is at issue for older cases), whichever comes first.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"73","t":"The clerk","s":"The clerk’s office is open during business hours except judicial holidays and Saturdays. The clerk may enter certain orders without court direction, such as consent substitutions of attorneys, consent satisfactions, Rule 55(a) defaults, and orders for issuing process, and must promptly notify the judge, who may change them for cause. The clerk may also enter Rule 55(b) default judgments on the required affidavits and judgments on accepted Rule 68 offers, and must distribute a copy of every order or judgment to each party not in default for failure to appear.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"74","t":"Clerk’s books and records","s":"The clerk keeps a case file of all papers in date order, an alphabetical index of every civil case and party, a record of every final judgment or order, a posted calendar of hearings and trials distinguishing jury from nonjury cases, and other records the administrative director requires. Court records may not leave the clerk’s custody except as allowed, such as by court officers on a signed receipt. After final judgment and the time for appeal or review, models, diagrams, exhibits, and depositions are returned to the submitting party (or may be withdrawn earlier on a stipulation waiving appeal), and unreturned items may be destroyed or disposed of as the court approves; ex parte documents signed by the court must be promptly delivered to the clerk.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"75","t":"Record of proceedings; transcript as evidence","s":"The superior court must keep a stenographic or electronic record of all open-court proceedings, unless the parties with the judge’s approval agree otherwise, and of other proceedings required by rule or order. When earlier recorded testimony is admissible at a later trial, it may be proved by a transcript certified by the reporter or a court officer.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"76","t":"Form of papers","s":"Filed papers must be on 8-1/2 x 11 inch opaque white paper of at least sixteen pound weight, typed in black at least 12 point Courier (or another Appellate Rule 513.5(c) typeface), with footers and certificates no smaller than 10 point, double- or one-and-one-half spaced (with single-spaced headings, footnotes, and indented long quotations), printed on one side, two-hole punched at the top, and stapled at the upper left. Documents longer than one page need a footer with the title, case name, case number, and “page x of y,” exhibit pages must be marked (for example, “Ex. A, p. 1 of 10”) and confidential exhibits sealed, and the first page must carry contact information (in 10 to 12 point type) and a caption laid out as specified. Signers’ names (and Alaska Bar numbers for attorneys) must be typed under the signature, signatures must be in blue or black ink, judges’ names must be typed on orders, interlineations are allowed only by the court, and the clerk may reject noncompliant papers, though the assigned judge may allow departures in emergencies.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"77","t":"Motions","s":"Motions must be served with supporting evidence, a memorandum of points and authorities, and a proposed order (self-represented parties need not file a proposed order); an opposition is due 10 days after service, or 15 days for motions to dismiss, for summary judgment, or for judgment on the pleadings (or, if served before an answer is due, by the later of that period and the answer date), while rules with their own timing (such as Rules 65, 77(g), 88 and 89) control, and the movant’s reply is due within 5 days after service of the opposition. A party wanting oral argument must request it within 5 days after service of a responsive pleading or the deadline for one, whichever is earlier; argument is a matter of right on dispositive motions and motions for delivery or attachment, is otherwise discretionary, and must be set within 45 days of the request or ripeness, whichever is later. A separate motion for expedited consideration needs an affidavit, a good-faith conferral certification when parties have counsel, and proof of notice, and no ruling on the principal motion may issue without a response until at least 24 hours after service or actual notice, whichever is sooner (except under Rule 65(b) or similar authority). A motion for reconsideration, limited to 5 pages and stating specific grounds, is due within 10 days after notice of the ruling (never more than 10 days after notice of final judgment), gets no response unless the court asks, and is deemed denied if not decided within 30 days; frivolous or delaying motions or oppositions can draw costs, fees and other sanctions.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"78","t":"Findings, conclusions, judgments and orders","s":"Unless the court orders otherwise, counsel for the successful party prepares, files and serves proposed findings, conclusions, judgments and orders, and in child custody matters must do so within 10 days after the judge announces on the record who will prepare them. Other parties may file detailed written objections within 5 days after service, and the court may then hold a conference or sign the document as prepared or modified. The rule also requires notice to the Attorney General in Juneau of punitive damages awards, sets the format for stipulations and orders (which must state grounds when they extend time or continue a matter), and requires money judgments to follow Rule 58.2.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"79","t":"Costs: taxation and review","s":"The prevailing party recovers reasonable, necessarily incurred costs, but only the items listed in the rule (such as filing and service fees, deposition and transcript costs, witness and interpreter fees, computerized legal research, copying at no more than $.15 per page for in-house attorney copies, exhibit preparation, and limited travel for one attorney when no local attorney attends). An itemized, verified cost bill must be filed and served within 10 days after the date on the clerk’s certificate of distribution on the judgment or costs are waived; objections are due within 7 days after service of the bill and a reply within 5 days after the objection. The clerk taxes costs, and a motion for court review of the clerk’s award must be filed within 5 days after distribution of the award, with the clerk’s award presumed reasonable; costs are apportioned under Rule 82(e) in fault-apportionment cases.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"80","t":"Bonds and undertakings","s":"The clerk may approve bonds executed by approved surety companies; individual sureties must be Alaska residents worth the bond amount net of exempt property and debts (or, with more than two sureties, together worth twice the amount), and attorneys and court officers cannot be sureties. On 3 days’ notice an adverse party may have a surety examined under oath about its qualifications, and an attorney must certify a bond as examined and recommended for approval. By signing, a surety submits to the court’s jurisdiction, appoints the clerk as its agent for service, and may be held liable on motion without a separate lawsuit; a cash deposit may be made instead of a bond.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"81","t":"Attorneys","s":"Only Alaska Bar members may practice in Alaska courts, but a lawyer in good standing elsewhere may appear in a particular case on motion served on the Bar’s executive director, with a fee paid to the Bar, the name of and written consent from an associated Alaska Bar member, and a good-standing certificate dated within 60 days of the motion; local counsel must also sign all papers and has primary responsibility and superior authority. An appearance is for all purposes unless the attorney files a limited entry of appearance identifying the limitation by date, time period or subject matter, and a represented party may not act personally without a court-ordered substitution. An attorney may withdraw with substitute counsel, with the client’s express consent plus contact details and a list of deadlines, for good cause on motion (the client may request a hearing within 10 days of service), or by notice at the end of a limited appearance; an attorney is deemed withdrawn one year after the last filing once the judgment is final and appeal time has run or the appeal is resolved, unless a notice of continued representation is filed. Stipulations count only if made in open court or in a filed writing, argument on motions and non-merits hearings is limited to 15 minutes per party unless ordered otherwise, and disbarred, suspended or felony-convicted lawyers may not practice until reinstated.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"82","t":"Attorney’s fees","s":"The prevailing party in a civil case is awarded partial attorney’s fees: for a money judgment (plus any prejudgment interest) the court applies a schedule of 20% of the first $25,000, 10% of the next $75,000, 10% of the next $400,000 and 10% over $500,000 if contested with trial; 18%, 8%, 6% and 2% if contested without trial; and 10%, 3%, 2% and 1% if non-contested. If the prevailing party recovers no money judgment, it receives 30% of its reasonable actual fees necessarily incurred if the case went to trial and 20% of actual fees if resolved without trial (counting delegated paralegal, investigator and law clerk work), and on a default judgment the plaintiff gets the lesser of the schedule amount or reasonable actual fees, which the clerk may determine. The court may vary the award, explaining why, based on complexity, length of trial, reasonableness of rates, hours and number of attorneys, efforts to minimize fees, reasonableness of claims and defenses, vexatious or bad-faith conduct, proportionality of work to stakes, whether the award would be so onerous it deters similar litigants from using the courts, whether fees were driven by outside considerations such as deterring claims by others, and other equitable factors. Fees (including contractual or statutory fees) require a motion filed within 10 days after the date on the clerk’s certificate of distribution on the judgment or they are waived unless the court allows more time, default motions must state actual fees, fees are apportioned by fault in AS 09.17.080 cases, and the award does not set fees between attorney and client.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"83","t":"Fees of witnesses, physicians, interpreters and translators","s":"Fees and mileage for witnesses, physicians, interpreters and translators are governed by the rules for the administration of the courts (the Administrative Rules), not by this rule.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"84","t":"Change of name","s":"A name change starts with a verified petition giving the desired name and reasons, and the court sets a hearing at least 40 days after its order, with notice posted for four consecutive weeks beforehand on the Alaska Court System’s legal notice website unless the court excuses it for the petitioner’s safety. If there is no reasonable objection, the judgment authorizes the new name effective no sooner than 30 days after distribution, the judgment must be posted for one week within 10 days after distribution, and any ordered proof of publication is due within 20 days. For a minor, a parent or guardian petitions and both legal parents and any guardian must consent or be served at least 30 days before the hearing; if a parent or guardian objects, the change is granted only if in the child’s best interest, and the child’s wishes are considered. The rule does not apply to restoring a prior name in a divorce or dissolution.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"85","t":"Forcible entry and detainer","s":"Eviction actions follow ordinary civil procedure with special rules: the summons must be served at least 2 days before the eviction hearing, the hearing is set within 15 days after the complaint is filed unless ordered otherwise, and continuances longer than 2 days require the defendant to post an undertaking for accruing rent. Appearing at the eviction hearing is an appearance in the whole case, and mail service to the address from which the defendant was evicted is allowed only if the defendant’s current whereabouts are not readily ascertainable. The clerk may dismiss a case pending more than 180 days with no hearing scheduled and no default application filed, such dismissals are without prejudice, and any party may reopen as of right by written request within one year; superior court cases may be referred to district court.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"86","t":"Habeas corpus","s":"Habeas corpus actions follow the civil rules where applicable, starting with a verified complaint stating who is restrained, by whom, where, why the restraint is illegal, and that its legality has not already been decided on a prior writ. The court issues a writ, an order to show cause or a warrant unless relief plainly is not warranted, the custodian must answer under oath explaining its authority, failing to produce the prisoner or answer is contempt, and the court decides summarily, with notice to interested persons or the prosecutor before releasing someone held on process or criminal charges. An order to show cause, not a writ, issues first when a relative seeks custody of a child under 16, and claims that fit post-conviction relief are treated as Criminal Rule 35.1 applications.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"87","t":"Civil arrest","s":"A motion for an order of arrest in a civil action must be supported by an affidavit stating the facts justifying arrest. The order must state the bail amount, and the arresting officer must give the defendant copies of the order and supporting affidavits at the time of arrest.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"88","t":"Prejudgment delivery of personal property","s":"A plaintiff suing to recover personal property may move, with a detailed affidavit (description, ownership, value, defendant’s possession, no intent to defraud creditors, and no known bankruptcy), for its seizure before judgment. Except in ex parte cases, the defendant gets notice and a hearing held 3 to 7 business days after service, the plaintiff must show by a preponderance the probable validity of its claim and no reasonable probability of a successful defense, the plaintiff must post an undertaking, and the defendant can block or undo seizure by posting security (with alternatives for indigent defendants); the officer files a return within 20 days. Ex parte orders are allowed only for imminent concealment, destruction, removal or transfer, defendant’s written waiver, or government plaintiffs protecting a public interest, and last no more than 7 business days unless waived or extended by consent; a hearing-based order expires after 6 months unless a notice of readiness for trial is filed or judgment is entered, extendable on a motion made 10 to 60 days before expiration with at least 5 days’ notice.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"89","t":"Attachment","s":"After filing suit, a plaintiff (or any claimant) may seek prejudgment attachment of the defendant’s property under AS 09.40.010–.110, but only on a contract claim for money supported by an affidavit (existing debt, inadequate security, no intent to defraud creditors, no known bankruptcy) and only after posting a written undertaking. Except in ex parte cases, a hearing is held 3 to 7 business days after service, the plaintiff must prove by a preponderance the probable validity of its claim and no reasonable probability of a successful defense, the defendant can avoid attachment by posting security (with alternatives for indigent defendants), and wages cannot be attached before final judgment except as federal and state statutes allow. Ex parte writs are allowed only to obtain jurisdiction over a non-resident not otherwise reachable, for imminent flight, concealment or removal of property, on the defendant’s written waiver, or for government plaintiffs, and last no more than 7 business days unless extended; the rule also governs garnishee examination and judgments, and hearing-based writs expire after 6 months unless a notice of readiness for trial is filed or judgment is entered, extendable on motion made 10 to 60 days before expiration with at least 5 days’ notice. The administrative director may adopt alternative procedures for electronic executions.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"90","t":"Contempts","s":"A judge may punish contempt summarily if the judge certifies seeing or hearing the conduct in the court’s presence, with a signed order reciting the facts. For other contempts, on an ex parte motion with affidavits the court issues an order to show cause or a bench warrant, and the proceeding may be brought by the state or the aggrieved party, in the same case or separately. The court may allow a bond instead of arrest (bond recoveries go first to the aggrieved party’s damages and costs, with any remainder to the state), and after examining the accused and witnesses the court decides guilt or innocence.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"90.1","t":"Dissolution, divorce and legal separation actions","s":"A dissolution of marriage starts with a petition, verified by both spouses if filed jointly, that must be filed within 60 days after the first signature, and no divorce or dissolution may be heard on the merits within 30 days after filing absent good cause; corroborating residency witnesses are not required unless ordered. In disputed property division cases, each party must file and serve a table of all assets and liabilities, their marital or separate character, values and proposed disposition 5 days before trial. Social security numbers go only on a confidential information sheet, financial accounts may be identified by the last four digits (cards) or last three digits (bank accounts), QDROs must be filed with a redacted duplicate, a later divorce must give notice of a prior legal separation action, and legal separation begins with a complaint in superior court.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"90.2","t":"Settlements and judgments for minors","s":"A parent or guardian may settle a minor’s claim, but the release is not effective until the court approves it on a petition or motion describing the minor, the claim, insurance, injuries and why the settlement is fair; the court approves attorney’s fees and costs and may approve without a hearing if net proceeds do not exceed $25,000. After paying reasonable expenses, costs and fees, the remaining proceeds must be handled in the minor’s best interests, such as being held by a parent or guardian if no more than $10,000, placed in a trust, conservatorship or court-restricted insured account, or transferred under the Alaska Uniform Transfers to Minors Act. A probate master may hear and recommend approval, and judgments in favor of minors are disbursed the same way.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"90.3","t":"Child support awards","s":"When one parent has primary physical custody, support equals the non-custodial parent’s adjusted annual income (total income minus taxes, mandatory deductions, voluntary retirement contributions capped with mandatory ones at 7.5% of income, prior support obligations, work-related child care, the parent’s own health premiums up to 10% of income, and life insurance premiums up to $1,200) times 20% for one child, 27% for two, 33% for three, and 3% more for each additional child; for parents earning $30,000 or less, adjusted income is the lesser of that figure or total income minus $7,500, and potential income may be imputed to a parent who is voluntarily and unreasonably unemployed or underemployed. Shared custody (each parent having the children 30% to 70% of the year) is computed by cross-calculating each parent’s obligation, weighting by the other parent’s custody time, and multiplying the difference by 1.5 (capped at the primary-custody amount), with separate methods for divided and hybrid custody, and the court may reduce support by up to 75% during extended visitation of over 27 consecutive days. The percentages do not apply to adjusted income over $138,000, the minimum award is $50 per month ($600 per year), and the court may vary the award only for good cause shown by clear and convincing evidence of manifest injustice, stating its reasons in writing. Health insurance must be ordered if available at reasonable cost (presumed reasonable at no more than 5% of adjusted income) and is split equally, uncovered health expenses are split equally and reimbursed within 30 days (with reasonable uncovered expenses over $5,000 a year allocated by relative finances), each parent must file a sworn income statement and exchange income documents within 30 days of a request at most once a year, a calculated change of more than 15% presumes a material change for modification, arrears cannot be modified retroactively before service of the modification motion, and collection may be precluded for periods over six consecutive months in which the obligee agreed to the obligor having primary custody.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"90.4","t":"Proceedings to establish parentage","s":"When genetic testing is ordered under AS 25.20.050, the results must be served on all parties at least 20 days before any hearing or trial where they may be offered, with an affidavit from a qualified person on the affiant’s qualifications and the validity of the testing. Objections must be filed and served at least 10 days before the hearing or trial, and without a timely objection the results are admissible as evidence of paternity without foundation testimony.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"90.5","t":"Expedited relief from license actions for unpaid child support","s":"A person seeking expedited court relief from a Child Support Services Division decision against an occupational or driver’s license under AS 25.27.244 or 25.27.246 must file a petition on the court form, stating the statutory grounds and filed (original plus one copy) within 30 days after receiving CSSD’s decision, ideally with an explanation of no more than 5 pages, documents, a witness list and copies of the decision and support order. CSSD must respond within 15 days after service, the hearing is held 20 to 30 days after service, and a notice of change of judge is timely if filed within 5 days after notice of the judge assignment. The court decides de novo only whether a support order is in effect, whether the petitioner is the obligor, and whether the petitioner is in substantial compliance (owing less than four times the monthly obligation or making best possible efforts to pay arrears); other relief requires an administrative appeal under Appellate Rule 602.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"90.6","t":"Child custody investigators","s":"In custody, divorce and domestic violence cases the court may appoint a qualified custody investigator as an Evidence Rule 706 expert, who must disclose potential conflicts within 10 days of appointment and file a written report by the court’s deadline. The report is confidential unless the court orders otherwise and is deemed admitted into evidence on filing, but a party may require the investigator to testify by listing the investigator on its final witness list, and may depose the investigator after the report is done. A party asked to sign a records release must sign it or move for a protective order within 10 days, the investigator may not have ex parte contact with the court except for scheduling, and fees are split equally unless the court finds good cause otherwise.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"90.7","t":"Guardians ad litem in child custody proceedings","s":"In custody, support or visitation cases the court may appoint a guardian ad litem only when separate representation of the child’s best interests is necessary, such as when needed evidence would not otherwise be presented or the case is unusually complex, and the appointment order must include findings and specify duties, deadlines, duration and compensation. The guardian ad litem advocates the child’s best interests, is treated as a party, must disclose conflicts within 10 days of appointment, must investigate (or arrange a Rule 90.6 investigation), files a trial or hearing brief by a court-set deadline disclosing any conflict with the child’s preference, and generally may not testify except on uncontested matters, the value of services, or factual evidence unavailable elsewhere. Discovery from the guardian ad litem is limited, the child’s communications are confidential and privileged subject to exceptions (the attorney-client privilege does not apply), and private guardian ad litem fees are split equally unless the court finds good cause otherwise, with monthly billing unless ordered otherwise.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"90.8","t":"Expedited redistricting plan challenges","s":"An application under article VI, section 11 of the Alaska Constitution to compel the Redistricting Board to correct an error in its plan must be filed within 30 days after adoption of the final plan and proclamation and served on the Board, the Attorney General’s office and the Lieutenant Governor’s office. These cases have priority over all other matters, the court must decide no later than 120 days before the statutory filing deadline for the first statewide election under the plan, and the assigned judge must hold a scheduling conference within 10 days of the application. The record is the Board’s record plus any evidence the court allows (depositions may be used without the Rule 32(a)(3)(B) limits), and this rule overrides inconsistent civil rules.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"91","t":"Applicability of civil rules in general","s":"The writs of scire facias, quo warranto and mandamus are abolished, and relief formerly available through them is sought by ordinary action or motion under these rules. The civil rules also apply to court proceedings to enforce state agency subpoenas unless the court orders otherwise.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"92","t":"Construction of rules","s":"The rules are meant to provide for the efficient operation of Alaska’s courts. Where no procedure is specified, the court may proceed in any lawful manner consistent with the rules, the constitution and the common law.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"93","t":"Legal effect of rules","s":"Because the rules are adopted under the supreme court’s constitutional rulemaking power, they supersede inconsistent procedural provisions of any statute that was not enacted specifically to change a rule.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"94","t":"Relaxation of rules","s":"The court may relax or dispense with any of these rules in a case where it is clear that strict adherence would work an injustice.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"95","t":"Penalties","s":"For any rule violation, after reasonable notice and an opportunity to be heard, the court may withhold or assess costs or attorney’s fees against offending attorneys or parties. Separately, after notice, an opportunity to show cause and a hearing if requested, the court may fine an attorney for failing to comply with court rules up to $50,000 in cases filed on or after August 7, 1997, or up to $1,000 in cases filed before that date.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"97","t":"Title","s":"These rules may be cited as the Rules of Civil Procedure.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"98","t":"Effective date","s":"The rules take effect on a date set by supreme court order and govern all civil actions begun afterward and, where just and practicable, actions then pending.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"99","t":"Telephonic participation","s":"For good cause and absent substantial prejudice, the court may let parties, counsel, witnesses or the judge participate by telephone in any hearing or deposition, and must allow video or Internet testimony at the request of a deployed parent in a custody or visitation matter. The court designates before the hearing who arranges and pays for the call under Administrative Rule 48, the judge opens by reciting the case details and instructing speakers to identify themselves, a verbatim record is required, and public access must be preserved.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"},{"n":"100","t":"Mediation and other alternative dispute resolution","s":"Any time after a complaint is filed, a party may move for mediation (or the court may order it in non-family cases), but in domestic violence situations family-law mediation is limited by statute and mediation may never be ordered in AS 18.66.100–.180 protective order cases; costs are split equally unless apportioned otherwise, and each party may peremptorily challenge a court-appointed mediator once under Rule 42(c). Optional confidential mediation briefs are limited to 5 pages and due at least 3 days before mediation, all parties must attend the initial conference, a party may withdraw after the first joint and separate sessions, and mediation is confidential and inadmissible to the extent of Evidence Rule 408. The rule also allows early neutral evaluation, stipulated arbitration, judicial settlement conferences on motion, and, with court approval, referral to tribal courts, tribal councils, elders’ courts or ethnic organizations.","u":"https://courts.alaska.gov/rules/docs/civ.pdf"}]});
